window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This document gives a general description of artificial intelligence (AI). It tells you: What AI is. How AI is different from a usual computer program. How an AI system learns. How a chat AI makes text. The limits and risks of AI. You do not need special knowledge to read this document."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"what-ai-is","n":"2","t":"What AI is","x":"AI is a type of computer software. It does tasks that usually need a human mind. Examples of these tasks are: To identify a face in a photograph. To translate text from one language to a different language. To change speech into written text. To answer a question in a chat. To find a defect on a part from a camera image. A usual computer program obeys rules. A person writes these rules, one by one. An AI system is different. It finds its own rules. It finds these rules in a large quantity of examples. We call these examples data . Figure 1. In a usual program, a person writes the rules. In machine learning, the computer finds the rules in the data. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 1 of 8 Rules or learning? Asha has a mango farm near Ratnagiri. Each day, her workers sort thousands of mangoes into three boxes: ripe, not ripe, and damaged. This work is slow, and tired workers make errors. Asha's nephew writes a usual computer program. It obeys one rule: \"If the skin is yellow, the mango is ripe.\" The rule does not work well: Some types of ripe mango stay green. In the evening light, green mangoes look yellow. The rule cannot find small black spots of damage. Thus Asha decides to use AI. A model will learn the rules from many photographs that experienced workers sort. Think: why is it difficult to write rules for this task? Ripeness depends on many small signs together: the color, small spots, the shape, and the light in the photograph. A person cannot write a rule for each combination. A model can find these patterns in many examples. Part 2 →"},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"the-parts-of-ai","n":"3","t":"The parts of AI","x":"AI is a large area. It has smaller areas in it. Refer to Figure 2. Artificial intelligence is the full area. It includes all methods that make a computer do \"smart\" tasks. Machine learning (ML) is one part of AI. The computer learns from data. Deep learning is one part of ML. It uses a large neural network . Generative AI is one part of deep learning. It makes new text, images, sound, or code. A chat AI is an example. Each of these areas has its own module. Module 2 gives the mathematics that they use. Refer to Module 3, Machine learning , Module 4, Deep learning , and Module 5, Generative AI . Figure 2. Each area is a part of the larger area around it. Note All AI systems that you can use today are \"narrow\" AI. Each system can do only some types of tasks. A system that can do all human tasks does not exist at this time. We call that type of system AGI ."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"how-an-ai-system-learns","n":"4","t":"How an AI system learns","x":"The learning procedure is training . The result of training is a model . The model keeps what it learned as a very large set of numbers. We call these numbers weights . Training is a loop. The loop has these steps. Refer to Figure 3. The model receives an example from the data. The model makes a guess. The system compares the guess with the correct answer. The system calculates the error. The error shows how far the guess is from the correct answer. The system adjusts the weights by a small quantity. This makes the next error smaller. The loop starts again with the next example. The loop can occur millions or billions of times. After many loops, the guesses become accurate. For more information about training, refer to Module 3, Machine learning . Figure 3. The training loop. Each loop makes the model a small quantity more accurate. Three types of learning Type What the system gets Example Supervised learning Data with the correct answers. Emails with the label \"spam\" or \"not spam\". Unsupervised learning Data with no answers. The system finds groups and patterns. Put customers into groups that buy the same products. Reinforcement learning A reward for a good action and a penalty for a bad action. A program learns to play a game. It gets points when it wins."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"neural-networks","n":"5","t":"Neural networks","x":"Most modern AI uses a neural network. A neural network has many small units. We call these units neurons . The neurons are in layers. Refer to Figure 4. The input layer receives the data. For a photograph, each neuron receives the value of one pixel. The hidden layers do the calculations. The first layers find simple shapes, for example edges. The subsequent layers find larger shapes, for example eyes or ears. The output layer gives the result. For example: \"cat: 92%\". Each line between two neurons has a weight. A large weight makes the connection strong. A small weight makes it weak. Training adjusts all these weights. For more information about how a neural network calculates and learns, refer to Module 4, Deep learning . Figure 4. A neural network. Data goes from the left to the right. A real network can have billions of connections. Play video: But what is a neural network? (3Blue1Brown) Video 1. But what is a neural network? (3Blue1Brown, approx. 19 min) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"how-a-chat-ai-makes-text","n":"6","t":"How a chat AI makes text","x":"A chat AI uses a large language model (LLM). An LLM is trained on a very large quantity of text. This text comes from books, websites, and code. An LLM does one main task. It predicts the next word in a text. It does this task again and again. This makes full sentences and full answers. The procedure You write a question. We call this the prompt . The system cuts the text into small parts. We call these parts tokens . The model calculates a probability for each possible next token. The system selects one token. Usually it selects a token with a high probability. The system adds the token to the text. The system goes back to step 3. It stops at the end of the answer. Try it Cut text into tokens Write a sentence in the box. Look at how the system cuts it into tokens. Then try the examples below. Look at long words and numbers. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Figure 5. The model gives a probability to each possible next token. The numbers in this figure are only an example. An LLM does not look up facts in a database. It makes text that is probable, from the patterns that it learned. This is very useful. But it is also the cause of some errors. Refer to Section 8. The transformer Most LLMs use a design that has the name transformer . A transformer uses a method with the name attention . Attention lets each word \"look at\" the other words in the text. Thus the model knows that \"bank\" in \"river bank\" is different from \"bank\" in \"bank account\". For more information about transformers, refer to Module 4, Section 8 . For more information about chat AI, refer to Module 5, Generative AI . Play video: Large Language Models explained briefly (3Blue1Brown) Video 2. Large language models explained briefly (3Blue1Brown, approx. 8 min) Open on YouTube Play video: But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers (3Blue1Brown) Video 3. But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers (3Blue1Brown, approx. 27 min) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"where-people-use-ai","n":"7","t":"Where people use AI","x":"Area Use of AI Phones Face unlock, voice assistants, and photo improvement. Health Help doctors find problems in X-ray and scan images. Transport Find the fastest route. Help a driver keep the car in the lane. Industry Find defects on parts. Predict when a machine needs maintenance. Office work Write drafts, make summaries, translate, and write code. Games and AR/VR Control characters, make content, and identify objects in a camera view."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"limits-and-risks","n":"8","t":"Limits and risks","x":"Warning An AI system can give incorrect information that looks correct. Do not use AI output for medical, legal, financial, or safety decisions without a check by a qualified person. Know these limits of AI: Incorrect answers. An LLM can make facts, names, or references that do not exist. We call this a hallucination . Bias. A model learns from data that people made. If the data is not fair, the model can also be not fair. Old information. A model knows only the data from before its training stopped. Some systems can search the web to get new information. No real understanding. A model finds patterns. It does not have human experience of the world. Caution Do not give private data to an AI tool if you do not know how the tool keeps that data. Private data includes passwords, ID numbers, and company secrets. Procedure: use a chat AI safely Write a clear prompt. Tell the AI the task, the context, and the format of the answer. Read the answer carefully. Do a check of all important facts in a reliable source. If the answer is not correct, tell the AI the error and ask again. Make the final decision yourself."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"a-short-history-of-ai","n":"9","t":"A short history of AI","x":"AI is not new. Researchers started to work on it in the 1950s. There were periods of large hopes and periods of disappointment. The large changes came when three things became available together: large quantities of data, fast computer chips, and better methods. 1950–1969: the start 1970–1993: hopes and winters 1994–2011: learning from data 2012–2019: deep learning 2020 to now: generative AI 1950 Alan Turing asks: can machines think? In his paper “Computing Machinery and Intelligence”, Turing describes a test. Can a person find the difference between a computer and a human in a written conversation? 1956 The name “artificial intelligence” At a workshop at Dartmouth College in the USA, researchers use the name “artificial intelligence” for the new subject. John McCarthy suggested the name. 1958 The perceptron Frank Rosenblatt makes the perceptron, one of the first machines that learns from examples. It is a very simple neural network. 1966 ELIZA, an early chatbot Joseph Weizenbaum makes ELIZA. It uses simple rules to answer like a therapist. Many users think that it understands them, but it does not. 1974 The first “AI winter” The results are smaller than the promises. Governments give less money for AI research. We call this period an “AI winter”. 1980s Expert systems Companies use expert systems: programs with thousands of rules that people write. The rules are expensive to keep up to date. A second AI winter starts at the end of the 1980s. 1986 Backpropagation David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton, and Ronald Williams show how backpropagation trains neural networks with many layers. Refer to Module 4. 1997 A computer wins at chess IBM’s Deep Blue wins a chess match against the world champion, Garry Kasparov. 2009 ImageNet Researchers publish ImageNet, a data set with millions of labeled photographs. Large data sets make deep learning possible. 2012 Deep learning starts to win A deep CNN wins the ImageNet image competition with far fewer errors than the other methods. It trains on GPUs. 2016 AlphaGo DeepMind’s AlphaGo wins against Lee Sedol, one of the best players of the game Go. It uses deep learning and reinforcement learning. 2017 The transformer Google researchers publish “Attention Is All You Need”. The transformer becomes the base of most LLMs. 2020 GPT-3 OpenAI shows GPT-3, an LLM with 175 billion weights. It can do many tasks from a short prompt. 2022 ChatGPT and image generators Diffusion image generators become available to the public. In November, ChatGPT is released. It gets approximately 100 million users in two months. 2024 Laws for AI The European Union adopts the EU AI Act, the first large law about AI. Refer to Module 6. 2025 Reasoning models and agents Models that work through problems in steps, and AI agents that use tools, become common. Note Each new method in this list uses the methods before it. A chat AI in 2025 still uses neurons from 1958 and backpropagation from 1986."},{"p":"ai.html","m":1,"mt":"Artificial intelligence","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"AI is software that does tasks that usually need a human mind. Machine learning finds rules in data. A person does not write the rules. Training is a loop: guess, compare, adjust, repeat. A neural network has layers of neurons. Weights connect the neurons. A chat AI predicts the next token again and again. AI can make errors. Always do a check of important information."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of the mathematics that machine learning uses. It tells you: Why AI needs mathematics, and which parts it needs. How to describe data with statistics. How to calculate and use probabilities. How vectors and matrices keep and change data. How calculus tells a model which direction makes its errors smaller. Note If mathematics is new for you, read each example slowly, and use the activities. You can also use the quick path on the home page. It shows only the most important sections."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"why-ai-needs-mathematics","n":"2","t":"Why AI needs mathematics","x":"A model is a mathematical function. It changes input numbers into output numbers. Training changes the numbers inside the function until the output is good. Four parts of mathematics do this work: Part What it does Where the manual uses it Statistics Describes data and measures results. Data and evaluation. Refer to Module 3, Section 10 . Probability Measures how certain or uncertain something is. Predictions, next-token sampling, and temperature. Refer to Module 5, Section 5 . Linear algebra Keeps data as lists and tables of numbers, and calculates with them quickly. Features, embeddings, and the layers of neural networks. Refer to Module 4, Section 3 . Calculus Measures how fast a value changes. Gradient descent and backpropagation. Refer to Module 3, Section 8 ."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"variables-and-functions","n":"3","t":"Variables and functions","x":"A variable is a name for a value that can change. Mathematics usually uses letters, for example x or w . A function is a rule that changes each input into one output. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. A function. For the input 3, the function calculates 2 × 3 + 1 = 7. In the function f ( x ) = 2 x + 1, the numbers 2 and 1 are parameters . A model is a function with many parameters. In a neural network, the parameters are the weights and the biases. Training finds good values for them. The symbol Σ (sum) Machine learning frequently adds many values. The symbol Σ (the Greek letter sigma) means \"add all of these\". Σ x i = x 1 + x 2 + x 3 + … Example: for the values 2, 5, and 3, Σ x i = 2 + 5 + 3 = 10. The small i is a counter. It shows the position of each value in the list."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"statistics-describe-data","n":"4","t":"Statistics: describe data","x":"Before you train a model, you must know your data. Statistics gives a few numbers that describe many values. In this section, the data is the weight of seven mangoes from Asha's farm, in grams: 280, 290, 295, 300, 305, 310, 315 The center of the data The mean (average) is the sum of the values, divided by the number of values: 2,095 ÷ 7 ≈ 299 g. The median is the middle value when the values are in order: 300 g. The mode is the value that occurs most frequently. Here, each value occurs one time, so there is no mode. The spread of the data The range is the largest value minus the smallest value: 315 − 280 = 35 g. The variance shows how far the values are from the mean. For each value, calculate the difference from the mean, and multiply the difference by itself. The variance is the mean of these results. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance. It has the same unit as the data. Here it is approximately 11 g. Thus most mangoes weigh between 288 g and 310 g. standard deviation = √( Σ(x i − mean)² ÷ n ) Warning An outlier is a value that is very different from the others. One outlier can change the mean very much. If a worker types 2,000 g in place of 315 g, the mean becomes 540 g. The median stays at 300 g. Always look for outliers before you train a model. Try it Calculate the statistics Change the numbers in the box, or select an example. Look at how the mean and the median change when you add an outlier. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Play video: Calculating the Mean, Variance and Standard Deviation, Clearly Explained (StatQuest) Video 1. Calculating the mean, variance and standard deviation (StatQuest) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"distributions","n":"5","t":"Distributions","x":"A distribution shows how frequently each value occurs. Many measurements in nature have a normal distribution . Its shape is like a bell. Most values are near the mean. Few values are far from the mean. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. A normal distribution of mango weights, with a mean of 300 g and a standard deviation of 11 g. 68% of the mangoes are within one standard deviation of the mean. In a normal distribution: Approximately 68% of the values are within 1 standard deviation of the mean. Approximately 95% are within 2 standard deviations. Approximately 99.7% are within 3 standard deviations. Standardization Features can have very different sizes. For example, the weight of a mango is approximately 300, but its ripeness score is between 0 and 1. Many models learn better when all features have a similar size. Standardization changes each value into a z-score: z = (x − mean) ÷ standard deviation Example: a mango of 322 g has z = (322 − 300) ÷ 11 = 2. It is 2 standard deviations heavier than the mean. This is unusual, but not an error. Play video: The Normal Distribution, Clearly Explained (StatQuest) Video 2. The normal distribution, clearly explained (StatQuest) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"correlation","n":"6","t":"Correlation","x":"Correlation shows how strongly two values change together. The correlation coefficient r is between −1 and +1. Refer to Figure 3. r near +1: when one value increases, the other also increases. Example: the length and the weight of a mango. r near −1: when one value increases, the other decreases. Example: the days after picking and the firmness of a mango. r near 0: there is no straight-line relation. Example: the weight of a mango and the day of the week. Figure 3. Positive correlation, negative correlation, and no correlation. Each point is one mango. Caution Correlation does not show cause. In summer, people buy more ice cream, and more people swim and have accidents in the water. The two values have a strong correlation, but ice cream does not cause the accidents. The hot weather causes both. A model learns correlations. It does not know causes."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"probability","n":"7","t":"Probability","x":"A probability is a number between 0 and 1. It shows how possible an event is. 0 means \"impossible\". 1 means \"certain\". We write P(A) for \"the probability of A\". A usual die has 6 sides. P(you throw a 6) = 1 ÷ 6 ≈ 0.17. The probability that A does not occur is 1 − P(A). P(no 6) = 1 − 0.17 = 0.83. If two events do not have an effect on each other, they are independent . The probability that both occur is P(A) × P(B). Two coins both show heads: 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25. Conditional probability A conditional probability is the probability of A when you know that B occurred. We write P(A | B), and we say \"the probability of A, given B\". Bayes' rule Bayes' rule changes a probability when you get new information: P(A | B) = P(B | A) × P(A) ÷ P(B) Example: 3% of Asha's mangoes are damaged. The checker finds 90% of the damaged mangoes. But it also puts 5% of the good mangoes into the damaged box. If a mango is in the damaged box, what is the probability that it is really damaged? Think of 1,000 mangoes: 30 mangoes are damaged. The checker finds 90% of them: 27 mangoes. 970 mangoes are good. The checker puts 5% of them in the damaged box: approximately 49 mangoes. The damaged box contains 27 + 49 = 76 mangoes. Only 27 of them are damaged. P(damaged | in the damaged box) = 27 ÷ 76 ≈ 0.36. Thus only 36% of the mangoes in the damaged box are really damaged. This is the precision of the checker. Refer to Module 3, Section 10 . Note When an event is rare, even a good test gives many false alarms. This is true for disease tests, fraud detection, and damaged mangoes. Try it See Bayes' rule in 1,000 mangoes Move the sliders. Each square is one mango. Look at how many mangoes in the damaged box are really damaged. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Play video: Bayes theorem, the geometry of changing beliefs (3Blue1Brown) Video 3. Bayes theorem, the geometry of changing beliefs (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"probability-in-ai-models","n":"8","t":"Probability in AI models","x":"From scores to probabilities: softmax A classification model first calculates a score for each category. A function with the name softmax changes the scores into probabilities: Calculate e score for each score. The number e is approximately 2.718. Add all the results. Divide each result by the sum. Category Score e score Probability Ripe 2 7.39 7.39 ÷ 11.11 = 0.67 Not ripe 1 2.72 2.72 ÷ 11.11 = 0.24 Damaged 0 1.00 1.00 ÷ 11.11 = 0.09 The sum of the probabilities is 1. A chat AI uses the same function for the next token. The temperature divides the scores before softmax. A low temperature makes the largest probability larger. Refer to Module 5, Section 5 . The loss for a probability During training, the loss measures how much probability the model gave to the correct answer. A frequent loss is cross-entropy : loss = −log(probability of the correct answer). The correct answer gets 0.9: loss = −log(0.9) ≈ 0.11. This is a small loss. The correct answer gets 0.1: loss = −log(0.1) ≈ 2.30. This is a large loss. Thus the model gets a large loss when it is sure but wrong. Training makes this loss smaller."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"vectors","n":"9","t":"Vectors","x":"A vector is an ordered list of numbers. One example in the data is a vector of its features. For example, a mango with a weight of 300 g, a length of 12 cm, and a ripeness score of 0.8 is the vector [300, 12, 0.8]. An embedding is also a vector. Refer to Module 4, Section 7 . Calculations with vectors Add two vectors: add the numbers in the same position. [1, 2] + [3, 1] = [4, 3]. Multiply a vector by a number: multiply each number. 2 × [1, 2] = [2, 4]. The length of a vector: √(sum of the squares). The length of [3, 4] is √(9 + 16) = 5. The dot product The dot product multiplies the numbers in the same positions, and then adds the results: a · b = a 1 b 1 + a 2 b 2 + … Example: [1, 2] · [3, 1] = 1 × 3 + 2 × 1 = 5. A neuron calculates a dot product: its inputs · its weights. Refer to Module 4, Section 3 . Cosine similarity Cosine similarity measures if two vectors point in the same direction. It is the dot product divided by the two lengths: cosine similarity = (a · b) ÷ (|a| × |b|) The result is between −1 and +1. A value near 1 means \"very similar\". Search systems with embeddings use this measurement to find text with the same meaning. Try it Change two vectors Move the sliders to change the direction and the length of the two vectors. Look at the dot product and the cosine similarity. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Play video: Vectors, Chapter 1, Essence of linear algebra (3Blue1Brown) Video 4. Vectors, chapter 1 of Essence of linear algebra (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"matrices","n":"10","t":"Matrices","x":"A matrix is a table of numbers, with rows and columns. A data set is a matrix: each row is one example, and each column is one feature. A matrix with 6,000 rows and 3 columns has the shape 6,000 × 3. A matrix times a vector To multiply a matrix by a vector, calculate the dot product of each row with the vector. Each row gives one number. Refer to Figure 4. Figure 4. A matrix of weights times a vector of inputs. The first row is the neuron from Module 4, Figure 2. The result is the total of each neuron before its bias. A layer of a neural network does exactly this calculation. Each row of the matrix contains the weights of one neuron. Then the layer adds the biases and applies the activation function: outputs = activation( W · x + b ) A large model does billions of these multiplications for each answer. GPUs can do many of them at the same time. This is why AI uses GPUs."},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"calculus-rates-of-change","n":"11","t":"Calculus: rates of change","x":"Slope The slope of a straight line shows how much the output changes when the input increases by 1. For f ( x ) = 2 x + 1, the slope is 2. The derivative A curve does not have one slope. Its slope changes at each point. The derivative is the slope of the curve at one point. For f ( x ) = x ², the derivative is f ′( x ) = 2 x : At x = 3, the slope is 6. The curve goes up quickly. At x = −1, the slope is −2. The curve goes down. At x = 0, the slope is 0. This is the bottom of the curve. Gradient descent uses this information. If the slope of the loss is positive, a smaller weight decreases the loss. If the slope is negative, a larger weight decreases the loss. Thus each step moves the weight in the direction opposite to the slope: new weight = weight − learning rate × slope Refer to Module 3, Section 8 . Try it Find the slope of a curve Move the point along the curve. Look at the tangent line and its slope. Find the point where the slope is 0. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. The gradient A model has many weights, not one. A partial derivative is the slope for one weight, when the other weights do not change. The gradient is the vector of all the partial derivatives. It points in the direction in which the loss increases fastest. Gradient descent goes in the opposite direction. The chain rule In a neural network, each layer uses the output of the previous layer. The chain rule finds the slope through many steps: multiply the slopes of the steps. if y depends on u, and u depends on x: (slope of y for x) = (slope of y for u) × (slope of u for x) Example: three gears. Gear A turns gear B 2 times faster. Gear B turns gear C 3 times faster. Thus gear C turns 2 × 3 = 6 times faster than gear A. Backpropagation uses the chain rule from the last layer back to the first layer. Refer to Module 4, Section 4 . Play video: The essence of calculus (3Blue1Brown) Video 5. The essence of calculus (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"mathematics.html","m":2,"mt":"Mathematics for machine learning","id":"summary","n":"12","t":"Summary","x":"A model is a function with parameters. Training finds good values for the parameters. The mean, the median, and the standard deviation describe data. Look for outliers. Many measurements have a normal distribution. Standardization gives features a similar size. Correlation shows that two values change together. It does not show cause. Bayes' rule changes a probability with new information. Rare events give many false alarms. Softmax changes scores into probabilities. Cross-entropy measures the loss. Vectors and matrices keep data. A neural layer multiplies a matrix by a vector. The derivative is the slope. Gradient descent moves opposite to the slope. The chain rule makes backpropagation possible."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of machine learning (ML). It tells you: The steps that make an ML system. The types of data that ML uses. The three types of learning. How a model finds its errors and makes them smaller. How to measure the quality of a model. Some common ML methods."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"what-machine-learning-is","n":"2","t":"What machine learning is","x":"Machine learning is one part of AI. In ML, a computer learns from examples. A person does not write the rules. The computer finds the rules in the data. Think of a person who learns to identify ripe mangoes. Nobody gives that person a list of rules. The person looks at many mangoes. Each time, somebody tells the person \"ripe\" or \"not ripe\". After many examples, the person can identify a ripe mango alone. ML uses the same principle. Play video: A Gentle Introduction to Machine Learning (StatQuest) Video 1. A gentle introduction to machine learning (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"the-machine-learning-workflow","n":"3","t":"The machine learning workflow","x":"A team does these steps to make an ML system. Refer to Figure 1. Define the problem. Write what the system must predict. Collect data. Get many examples of the problem. Prepare the data. Remove errors and examples that occur two times. Divide the data into three sets. Refer to Section 4. Select a type of model. Train the model with the training data. Evaluate the model with data that it did not see before. Put the model into use. Monitor its results. If the model is not sufficiently accurate at step 7, adjust it and train it again. When new data becomes available, go back to step 2. Figure 1. The ML workflow. The dashed lines show the two loops back."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"data","n":"4","t":"Data","x":"Features and labels ML data is usually a table. Each row is one example. Each column gives one item of information about the example. We call these columns features . The value that the model must predict is the label . Refer to Figure 2. Features: the inputs Label: the answer Area (m²) Bedrooms Floor Distance to station (km) Price (₹ lakh) 650 2 4 0.8 142 420 1 2 2.5 78 980 3 11 1.2 235 700 2 7 3.1 ? Figure 2. Data to predict the price of a flat. The blue columns are features. The green column is the label. The model must predict the \"?\" in the last row. All values are only examples. Warning Bad data makes a bad model. If the data has errors, gaps, or bias, the model learns them. Always do a check of the data before training. Three sets of data Before training, divide the data into three sets. Refer to Figure 3. Training set. The model learns from this set. Validation set. You use this set to adjust the model during development. Test set. You use this set one time, at the end. It shows the real accuracy of the model. Figure 3. A usual division of the data. The percentages can change for different projects. Caution Do not let the model see the test set during training. If it sees the test set, the test result will be too good. It will not show the real accuracy. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 2 of 8 Collect the data The team does the steps of the ML workflow. Refer to Section 3. Define the problem. Put each photograph into one of three categories: ripe, not ripe, or damaged. This is classification. Collect data. A camera above the sorting table takes 6,000 photographs. Add labels. Two experienced workers examine each mango. Their decision is the label. Divide the data. 4,200 photographs for training, 900 for validation, and 900 for the test. The features are the pixels of each photograph. The label is the category. Think: the two workers do not agree about 300 mangoes. What must the team do with these photographs? Ask a third expert to do a check of these photographs, or remove them. Labels with errors teach the model errors. Refer to the warning above. ← Part 1 Part 3 →"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"supervised-learning","n":"5","t":"Supervised learning","x":"In supervised learning, each example has a label. The model learns the relation between the features and the label. There are two main types. Refer to Figure 4. Regression predicts a number. Example: the price of a flat. Classification predicts a category. Example: \"spam\" or \"not spam\". Figure 4. Regression finds a line that predicts a number. Classification finds a boundary that divides the categories."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"unsupervised-learning","n":"6","t":"Unsupervised learning","x":"In unsupervised learning, the examples do not have labels. The model finds patterns alone. A usual task is clustering . The model puts similar examples into groups. Refer to Figure 5. Example: a shop has data about its customers. Nobody knows the types of customer. The model finds three groups: students, families, and office workers. Then the shop can send different offers to each group. Figure 5. Clustering. The model finds the groups. It does not know the names of the groups. A person gives the names after. Try it Watch the model find groups Select One step again and again. Look at how the centers (×) move until they stop. Then add points, change the number of groups, or try a new random start. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"reinforcement-learning","n":"7","t":"Reinforcement learning","x":"In reinforcement learning, an agent learns by trial and error. The agent is in an environment . Refer to Figure 6. The agent sees the current condition of the environment. We call this the state . The agent does an action. The environment changes. The agent gets a reward . A good action gives a high reward. A bad action gives a low reward. The agent changes its behavior to get more reward in the future. Robots, game programs, and some parts of chat AI training use this method. Figure 6. The reinforcement learning loop. The agent tries to get the maximum total reward."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"how-a-model-makes-its-errors-smaller","n":"8","t":"How a model makes its errors smaller","x":"The loss During training, the system measures the errors of the model. The result is one number. We call this number the loss . A high loss shows many errors. A low loss shows few errors. The objective of training is to make the loss as low as possible. Gradient descent Most models use a method with the name gradient descent . Think of a person on a hill in fog. The person cannot see the bottom. But the person can feel the slope under the feet. The person takes a step downhill. Then the person feels the slope again and takes the next step. Gradient descent does the same thing with the loss. Refer to Figure 7. Calculate the loss for the current weights. Calculate the slope of the loss. This slope is the gradient . Move the weights a small step in the downhill direction. Do steps 1 to 3 again until the loss stops decreasing. The size of each step is the learning rate . Neural networks also use gradient descent. They use a method with the name backpropagation to calculate the gradient. Refer to Module 4, Section 4 . Figure 7. Gradient descent. Each green step moves downhill. The steps become smaller near the bottom because the slope becomes smaller. Try it Find the bottom of the loss Set the learning rate and the start weight. Then select One step or Run 15 steps . Try each learning rate below and look at what the steps do. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Caution Select the learning rate carefully. If it is too large, the model jumps over the bottom and does not become accurate. If it is too small, training is very slow. Play video: Gradient descent, how neural networks learn (3Blue1Brown) Video 2. Gradient descent, how neural networks learn (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"overfitting-and-underfitting","n":"9","t":"Overfitting and underfitting","x":"A good model works correctly on new data. It does not only remember the training data. Refer to Figure 8. Underfitting . The model is too simple. It does not find the pattern. It makes errors on the training data and on new data. Good fit. The model finds the general pattern. It works correctly on new data. Overfitting . The model is too complex. It remembers each training example, also the random errors. It makes many errors on new data. Overfitting is like a student who memorizes the answers to old exam papers. That student cannot answer a new question. Figure 8. The same data with three different models. Only model B will give good predictions for new data. To prevent overfitting: Use more training data. Use a simpler model. Stop training when the validation loss starts to increase. Play video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: Bias and Variance (StatQuest) Video 3. Machine learning fundamentals: bias and variance (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"how-to-measure-a-model","n":"10","t":"How to measure a model","x":"After training, measure the model with the test set. For a classification model, use a confusion matrix . It compares the predictions with the correct answers. Refer to Figure 9. Figure 9. A confusion matrix for a spam filter. The filter examined 100 emails. Green cells are correct. Red cells are errors. Measurement Question that it answers When it is important Accuracy How many of all predictions are correct? When the categories have approximately the same size. Precision When the model says \"spam\", how frequently is it correct? When a false alarm is expensive. Example: a good email goes to the spam folder. Recall Of all the real spam, how much does the model find? When a missed item is dangerous. Example: a test for a disease. Note High accuracy is not always good. Think of a disease that occurs in 1 of 100 persons. A model that always says \"no disease\" is 99% accurate. But it finds zero sick persons. Thus, also look at precision and recall. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 3 of 8 Test the model The first model is 97% accurate on the test set. Asha is happy. But her customers complain about damaged mangoes in their boxes. The team looks at the confusion matrix. Only 3% of the mangoes are damaged, and the model finds very few of them. The recall for damaged mangoes is only 40%. A damaged mango in a box is expensive: the customer sends back the full box. Thus the recall for damaged mangoes is the most important measurement. The team collects more photographs of damaged mangoes and trains the model again. Now the recall for damaged mangoes is 92%. Think: why was 97% accuracy not a good measurement for this model? Most mangoes are not damaged. A model can be very accurate and still miss most of the damaged mangoes. Refer to the note above. ← Part 2 Part 4 → Play video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: The Confusion Matrix (StatQuest) Video 4. Machine learning fundamentals: the confusion matrix (StatQuest) Open on YouTube | Find by title"},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"common-machine-learning-methods","n":"11","t":"Common machine learning methods","x":"There are many types of ML model. Each type is good for some tasks. This table shows some common types. Method Type How it works Example use Linear regression Supervised, regression Finds the straight line that is nearest to the data points. Predict the price of a flat. Logistic regression Supervised, classification Calculates the probability that an example is in a category. Predict if a customer will stop a subscription. Decision tree Supervised, both Asks a sequence of yes/no questions about the features. Approve or refuse a loan application. Random forest Supervised, both Uses many decision trees. Each tree gives a vote. Find fraud in card payments. k-nearest neighbors Supervised, both Looks at the most similar examples in the training data. Recommend similar products. k-means Unsupervised, clustering Puts the data into k groups around k center points. Put customers into groups. Neural network All types Many layers of connected neurons. Refer to Module 4, Deep learning . Images, speech, text, and chat AI."},{"p":"machine-learning.html","m":3,"mt":"Machine learning","id":"summary","n":"12","t":"Summary","x":"In ML, the computer finds the rules in data. The ML workflow has eight steps, from the problem definition to monitoring. Features are the inputs. The label is the correct answer. Divide the data into training, validation, and test sets. Supervised learning uses labels. Unsupervised learning finds groups. Reinforcement learning uses rewards. Gradient descent makes the loss smaller, step by step. A good model works on new data. Prevent overfitting. Measure a model with accuracy, precision, and recall."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of deep learning. It tells you: What deep learning is, and how it is different from classic machine learning. How one neuron calculates its output. How a deep network learns with backpropagation. The main types of neural network. How a network finds patterns in images. How a network changes words into numbers. How a transformer uses attention. How to train a deep network in practice."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"what-deep-learning-is","n":"2","t":"What deep learning is","x":"Deep learning is a part of machine learning. It uses neural networks with many layers. The word \"deep\" refers to the number of layers. A deep network can have from ten to more than one hundred layers. In classic machine learning, a person usually selects the features. For example, to identify a cat, a person can tell the model to measure the shape of the ears. In deep learning, the network finds the useful features in the raw data. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. In classic ML, a person selects the features. In deep learning, the layers of the network learn the features from the raw data. Deep learning is the best method for data that does not have a clear structure, for example images, sound, and text. Item Classic machine learning Deep learning Features A person selects them. The network finds them. Quantity of data Hundreds to thousands of examples. Thousands to billions of examples. Computer power A usual computer. Special chips (GPUs). Refer to Section 9. Explanation of results Usually easy. Difficult. Best for Tables of numbers. Images, sound, and text. Note For data in tables, classic methods are frequently as accurate as deep learning. They are also faster and easier to explain. Refer to Module 3, Section 11 ."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"inside-a-neuron","n":"3","t":"Inside a neuron","x":"Module 1 tells you that a neural network has neurons in layers. Each neuron does a small calculation. Refer to Figure 2. The neuron receives numbers from the previous layer. It multiplies each number by its weight. It adds all the results. It adds one more number. We call this number the bias . It applies an activation function to the total. It sends the result to the neurons in the next layer. Figure 2. One neuron. It multiplies each input by its weight, adds the results and the bias, and applies the activation function. The numbers are only an example. The most common activation function is ReLU . ReLU changes negative numbers to zero. It does not change positive numbers. Try it Change the weights of a neuron This is the neuron from Figure 2. Move the weights and the bias. Look at the total and at the output after ReLU. Then try the challenges. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Note Without an activation function, many layers act the same as one layer. The activation function lets the network learn complex patterns, for example curves, and not only straight lines."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-deep-network-learns","n":"4","t":"How a deep network learns","x":"A deep network learns with gradient descent. Refer to Module 3, Section 8 . But a deep network has millions or billions of weights. The system must find the gradient for each weight. A method with the name backpropagation does this quickly. Refer to Figure 3. The data goes through the layers, from the input to the output. The network makes a prediction. We call this the forward pass . The system compares the prediction with the label. It calculates the loss. The system goes back through the layers, from the output to the input. It calculates how much each weight caused the error. We call this the backward pass. The system changes each weight by a small quantity, in the direction that decreases the loss. The system does these steps again with the next group of examples. Figure 3. Backpropagation. The forward pass makes a prediction. The backward pass sends the error back through the layers. The system does not use all the training data at one time. It uses small groups of examples. We call each group a batch . One pass through all of the training data is an epoch . Training usually needs many epochs. Play video: Backpropagation, intuitively (3Blue1Brown) Video 1. Backpropagation, intuitively (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"types-of-neural-network","n":"5","t":"Types of neural network","x":"There are different designs of neural network. Each design is good for a different type of data. Type Data How it works Example use Feedforward network Numbers in a table The data goes through the layers in one direction. Predict a price from features. CNN Images and video Small filters move across the image and find patterns. Refer to Section 6. Face ID. Find defects on parts. RNN Sequences, for example sound or sensor data It reads one item at a time. It keeps a memory of the previous items. Older speech and translation systems. Transformer Text, and now also images and sound Attention connects all parts of the input. Refer to Section 8. Chat AI, translation, and image generation. Transformers have replaced RNNs for most text tasks."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-network-finds-patterns-in-images","n":"6","t":"How a network finds patterns in images","x":"A photograph has millions of pixels. A usual network would need too many weights. A convolutional neural network (CNN) solves this problem. A CNN uses small filters . A filter is a small grid of weights, for example 3 × 3. The filter moves across the image, one position at a time. At each position, it calculates one number. The result is a new grid that we call a feature map . The feature map shows where the filter found its pattern. Refer to Figure 4. Figure 4. A CNN. This filter finds vertical edges. The high numbers in the feature map show where the image changes from light to dark. The same filter is used at all positions. Thus a CNN needs fewer weights. It can also find a pattern in any part of the image. A CNN has many layers of filters: The first layers find edges and lines. The middle layers find shapes, for example circles and corners. The last layers find parts of objects, for example eyes or wheels. The output layer gives the result, for example \"cat: 92%\". Training finds the values in the filters. A person does not design them. Play video: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) explained (deeplizard) Video 2. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) explained (deeplizard) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-network-changes-words-into-numbers","n":"7","t":"How a network changes words into numbers","x":"A neural network can use only numbers. Thus the system must change words, images, and other items into numbers. An embedding is a list of numbers that shows the meaning of an item. We call a list of numbers a vector . The network learns the embeddings during training. Items with similar meanings get similar numbers. Thus their embeddings are near each other. Refer to Figure 5. Figure 5. Embeddings. Words with similar meanings are near each other. A real embedding has hundreds or thousands of numbers. This figure shows only two numbers. Systems use embeddings to: Find text with the same meaning, not only the same words. Refer to Module 5, Section 8 . Recommend similar products, films, or songs. Put similar documents into groups. Start the calculation in a transformer. Refer to Section 8."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"how-a-transformer-uses-attention","n":"8","t":"How a transformer uses attention","x":"Module 1 tells you that most LLMs use a transformer. This section tells you how a transformer works. The meaning of a word depends on the other words in the text. Look at the sentence \"The animal did not cross the street because it was tired\". Here, the word \"it\" refers to the animal. In \"... because it was too wide\", the word \"it\" refers to the street. A method with the name attention solves this problem. For each token, attention calculates how important each other token is. Then it adds information from the important tokens into this token. Refer to Figure 6. Figure 6. Attention. The model learns that \"it\" refers to \"animal\". A real model calculates this for each token and in many layers. The numbers are only an example. The steps in a transformer The system cuts the text into tokens. The system changes each token into an embedding. It also adds information about the position of each token. In an attention layer, each token collects information from the other tokens. In a feedforward layer, each token goes through a small neural network. The model does steps 3 and 4 again in many layers. A large model can have more than 100 layers. The last layer gives the probability of each possible next token. Refer to Module 1, Section 6 . A transformer does the calculations for all tokens at the same time. Thus it can use the power of GPUs well. This is one reason why transformers replaced the older designs. Play video: Attention in transformers, step-by-step (3Blue1Brown) Video 3. Attention in transformers, step by step (3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"deep-learning-in-practice","n":"9","t":"Deep learning in practice","x":"Computer power Deep learning needs very many calculations. A GPU is a computer chip that does many calculations at the same time. Small networks can train on one GPU. Large language models train on thousands of GPUs for weeks or months. Prevent overfitting A deep network has very many weights. Thus it can easily remember the training data. Refer to Module 3, Section 9 . Use these methods to prevent overfitting: Dropout . During training, the system switches off some random neurons. Thus the network cannot depend on a small number of neurons. Data augmentation . The system makes more training examples from the existing examples. For example, it turns, flips, or crops each image. Early stopping. The system stops training when the validation loss starts to increase. Transfer learning To train a large network from zero, you need very much data and money. Transfer learning is a different method: Start with a network that is already trained on a large data set. For example, a network that is trained on millions of photographs. Train it a little more on your small data set. For example, 500 photographs of defects on your parts. The network keeps its general knowledge, for example how to find edges and shapes. It only learns the new task. Generative AI uses the same principle. Refer to Module 5, Section 4 . 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 4 of 8 A better model for photographs The model from Part 3 is a classic ML model. A person selected its features, for example the average color. It cannot see small spots well. The team changes to a CNN. They use transfer learning: Start with a CNN that is trained on millions of general photographs. Train it more on the 6,000 mango photographs. Use data augmentation: turn and flip the photographs, and change their brightness. The new model finds small spots and works in different light. The recall for damaged mangoes is now 97%. A buyer asks: \"Why did the model put this mango in the damaged box?\" The team cannot easily answer. Thus a worker does a check of each mango in the damaged box. Think: why does data augmentation with changes of brightness help this model? The light on the farm is different in the morning and in the evening. When the model sees training photographs with many types of light, it learns to ignore the light and to look at the mango. ← Part 3 Part 5 → Warning It is difficult to know why a deep network gives an answer. Do not use a deep network alone for decisions that have a large effect on people, for example a loan or a medical diagnosis. A qualified person must do a check of the decision."},{"p":"deep-learning.html","m":4,"mt":"Deep learning","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers. The network finds the features in the raw data. Each neuron multiplies its inputs by weights, adds a bias, and applies an activation function. Backpropagation finds the gradient for each weight. Gradient descent then adjusts the weights. CNNs use filters to find patterns in images. Embeddings change items into lists of numbers. Similar items have similar embeddings. Transformers use attention to connect each token to the other tokens. Transfer learning gives good results with less data."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of generative AI. It tells you: What generative AI is, and how it is different from predictive AI. The types of content that generative AI can make. How a large language model is made. How a model selects its output, and how you can control it. How an image generator makes a picture. How to write a good prompt. How to connect a model to your documents and to tools. The risks of generative AI, and how to use it responsibly."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"what-generative-ai-is","n":"2","t":"What generative AI is","x":"Most models in Module 3 are predictive. A predictive model looks at an input. Then it gives a label or a number. For example, it tells you if an email is spam. Generative AI is different. It makes new content. This content did not exist before. The model learns the patterns in its training data. Then it uses these patterns to make new examples that are similar to the data. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. A predictive model selects an answer. A generative model makes new content from a description. A large generative model can do many different tasks. We call this type of model a foundation model . Companies use one foundation model in many products, for example chat, writing help, code help, and search. Note Generative AI does not copy text or pictures from a database. It makes new output from the patterns that it learned. But sometimes the output can be very similar to an item in the training data."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"types-of-generative-ai","n":"3","t":"Types of generative AI","x":"Generative models can make different types of content. We call each type of content a modality . Output Examples of tasks Usual type of model Text Answer questions, write drafts, make summaries, and translate. Large language model (LLM) Code Write, explain, and repair program code. LLM that is also trained on code Images Make a picture from a text description. Change a part of a photograph. Diffusion model. Refer to Section 6. Sound and speech Change text into speech. Make music. Make a copy of a voice. Transformer or diffusion model Video Make short video clips from a text or from an image. Diffusion model 3D and design Make 3D models, product designs, and game content. Diffusion model or transformer A multimodal model can use more than one modality. For example, it can receive a photograph and a question. Then it writes an answer about the photograph."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-a-large-language-model-is-made","n":"4","t":"How a large language model is made","x":"A modern chat AI is made in three stages. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. The three stages that make a chat AI. Stage 1 uses the most data and the most computer time. Stage 1: Pretraining In pretraining , the model reads a very large quantity of text. Its task is to predict the next token. For more information about tokens, refer to Module 1, Section 6 . Most of these models are transformers. Refer to Module 4, Section 8 . Pretraining can take months. It uses thousands of special computer chips at the same time. We call these chips GPUs . Pretraining is the most expensive stage. The result is a base model . A base model knows much about language and about the world. But it only continues text. If you ask it a question, it can write more questions in place of an answer. Stage 2: Fine-tuning In fine-tuning , the model learns from a smaller set of examples. Each example has a question and a good answer. People write or check these examples. After fine-tuning, the model follows instructions. Fine-tuning is a type of transfer learning. Refer to Module 4, Section 9 . Stage 3: Human feedback The model writes two or more answers to the same question. People select the better answer. The model learns to give the type of answer that people prefer. This method uses reinforcement learning. Refer to Module 3, Section 7 . We call this method RLHF . After these three stages, the model is an assistant. It follows instructions, and it refuses some harmful requests. Note A model does not learn from your conversation immediately. Its weights change only when the company trains it again. But some companies use conversations as training data. Read the privacy settings of each AI tool that you use. Play video: Intro to Large Language Models (Andrej Karpathy) Video 1. Intro to large language models (Andrej Karpathy, approx. 1 h) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-a-model-selects-its-output","n":"5","t":"How a model selects its output","x":"Module 1 tells you that a model calculates a probability for each possible next token. Then the system selects one token. We call this selection sampling . If the system always selects the token with the highest probability, the text is predictable. But the text can become repetitive. If the system sometimes selects a token with a lower probability, the text is more varied. A setting with the name temperature controls this. Refer to Figure 3. Figure 3. At a low temperature, the most probable token is almost always selected. At a high temperature, other tokens are selected more frequently. The numbers are only an example. Temperature Effect Use it for Low (0 to 0.3) The answer is almost the same each time. Facts, calculations, code, and data extraction. Medium (0.5 to 0.8) The answer changes a little each time. Usual chat and writing. High (1.0 or more) The answer changes much each time. Errors are more probable. Ideas, poems, and stories. Try it Change the temperature Move the slider and look at the probabilities. Then select Make 10 answers . Do this at a low and at a high temperature, and compare the answers. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Note Because of sampling, the same prompt can give a different answer each time. This is usual. It is not a fault. The context window A model can use only a limited quantity of text at one time. We call this limit the context window . The context window includes your prompt, the documents that you give, the conversation until now, and the answer. The size of a context window is given in tokens. In English, 1,000 tokens are approximately 750 words. If a conversation is longer than the context window, the model cannot use the earliest parts."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-an-image-generator-works","n":"6","t":"How an image generator works","x":"Most image generators use a diffusion model . A diffusion model learns to remove noise from an image. Refer to Figure 4. Figure 4. A diffusion model. In training, the system adds noise to images. In generation, the model starts from noise and removes it. The prompt guides each step. A real model uses 20 to 50 steps. Training The system takes an image and its text description from the training data. The system adds a small quantity of random noise to the image. The model tries to predict the noise that the system added. The system compares the prediction with the real noise. Then it adjusts the weights. The system repeats these steps with more noise, millions of times. Generation You write a prompt. The system starts with an image that is only random noise. The model removes a small quantity of noise. The prompt tells the model what to find in the noise. The system repeats step 3 many times. A clear image appears. It is a new image that matches the prompt. Video generators use the same principle. They also keep the frames of the video consistent with each other. Play video: But how do AI images and videos actually work? (Welch Labs, on 3Blue1Brown) Video 2. But how do AI images and videos actually work? (Welch Labs, on 3Blue1Brown) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"how-to-write-a-good-prompt","n":"7","t":"How to write a good prompt","x":"The quality of the answer depends on the quality of the prompt. A good prompt gives the model the information that it needs. A good prompt can have five parts. Refer to Figure 5. Figure 5. The five parts of a good prompt. The task is always necessary. Add the other parts when they help. These examples show how to make a weak prompt better: Weak prompt Better prompt Write about rainbows. Explain how a rainbow forms, for students who are 10 years old. Use 5 short steps. Fix my code. This Python function must return the sum of a list. It gives an error when the list is empty. Find the cause and repair it. The code is below. Summarize this. Make a summary of this report for a manager. Use 5 bullet points. Include all costs and dates. Useful methods Give examples. Show one to three examples of the result that you want. We call this few-shot prompting . Ask for steps. For a problem with calculations or logic, ask the model to show its steps. Some new models do this automatically before they answer. We call them reasoning models . Divide a large task. Give the model one part of the task at a time. Let the model say \"I do not know\". Tell the model to say this when it does not have the information. This can decrease hallucinations. Try again. If the answer is not good, change the prompt. Tell the model what is wrong. Many AI products add hidden instructions before your prompt. We call these instructions the system prompt . The system prompt gives the model its role and its rules. Try it yourself Copy each prompt into any chat AI. Compare the answer with the description of a good answer. Exercise 1. Give the task, the readers, and the format. Explain how a rainbow forms, for students who are 10 years old. Use 5 short, numbered steps. Use no more than 100 words. Copy the prompt What a good answer looks like The answer has exactly 5 numbered steps and fewer than 100 words. It uses simple words: sunlight goes into raindrops, the light bends, it divides into colors, and the colors come back to your eyes. Now remove the readers and the format from the prompt, and compare the two answers. Exercise 2. Give examples (few-shot prompting). Put each review into one category: Positive, Negative, or Mixed. Review: The mangoes were sweet and arrived fast. Category: Positive Review: Half of the box was damaged. Category: Negative Review: Good taste, but very expensive. Category: Copy the prompt What a good answer looks like The answer is only one word: “Mixed”. The examples showed the model the format, so it does not write a long explanation. Exercise 3. Let the model say “I do not know”. Use only the text below to answer. If the text does not contain the answer, write: I do not know. Text: Asha stores ripe mangoes at 12 to 14 degrees Celsius. She does not wash them before storage. Question: How many days can the mangoes stay in storage? Copy the prompt What a good answer looks like A good answer is “I do not know”, because the text does not give the number of days. If the AI gives a number, it invented it. This is a hallucination. Refer to Section 9. Procedure: write a good prompt Write the task in one clear sentence. Give the context. Tell the model who will read the result, and why. Give the facts or the text that the model must use. Tell the model the format, the length, and the style of the answer. If possible, give one example of a good result. Send the prompt. Read the answer carefully. If the answer is not correct, change the prompt and send it again."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"connect-a-model-to-documents-and-tools","n":"8","t":"Connect a model to documents and tools","x":"A model knows only its training data. It does not know your company documents or the news of today. A method with the name RAG solves this problem. Refer to Figure 6. Figure 6. RAG. The system finds relevant text in your documents. It adds this text to the prompt. Then the model writes an answer from this text. A user asks a question. The system searches your documents for text that is related to the question. The search usually uses embeddings . Thus it finds text with the same meaning, not only the same words. Refer to Module 4, Section 7 . The system puts the question and the found text into one prompt. The model writes an answer from this text. The answer shows the sources. You can use them to do a check of the answer. RAG has these advantages: The answers can use new and private data. You can do a check of each source. There are usually fewer hallucinations. It is not necessary to train the model again when the documents change. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 5 of 8 A chatbot for farmers Asha writes a guide for other farmers: when to pick mangoes, how to keep them, and how to find damage. Many farmers telephone her with questions about the guide. Asha wants a chatbot to answer these questions. First, she tests a usual chat AI. It gives general answers. Sometimes it invents facts about storage temperatures. Then the team uses RAG: The system searches Asha's guide for the parts that are related to each question. It adds these parts to the prompt. The temperature setting is low, so that the answers are consistent. Each answer shows the page of the guide that it uses. Questions about pesticides and other chemicals always go to a person. An incorrect answer can cause harm. Think: why does the chatbot show the page of the guide with each answer? The farmer can do a check of the answer in the guide. This decreases the risk of hallucinations. ← Part 4 Part 6 → Tools and agents Some AI systems can use tools. For example, they can search the web, do calculations, run code, or send an email. An AI agent is a system that uses an LLM to plan steps. Then it uses tools to complete a task. Example task for an agent: \"Find three hotels in Pune for less than ₹5,000 per night. Make a table of their prices and ratings.\" Caution An agent can do actions in the real world. Make sure that a person approves each important action, for example a payment, an order, or an email. Play video: What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)? (IBM Technology) Video 3. What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)? (IBM Technology) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"risks-of-generative-ai","n":"9","t":"Risks of generative AI","x":"Warning Generative AI can make content that looks real but is false. This includes text, photographs, voices, and videos. Do not believe content only because it looks real. Know these risks: Hallucinations. A model can invent facts, quotes, and references. The text can sound correct and confident. Refer to Module 1, Section 8 . Deepfakes. A deepfake is a false image, video, or voice of a real person. Criminals use deepfakes for fraud. For example, a false voice of a family member asks for money. Copyright. Models are trained on content that people made. The rules about the use of this content are different in different countries. The output can also be similar to an existing work. Bias. Text and images from a model can repeat unfair ideas from the training data. Prompt injection. A web page or a document can contain hidden instructions for an AI. We call this prompt injection . These instructions can make an AI agent do something that you did not ask for. Private data. Data that you give to an AI tool can be kept by the company that makes the tool. Too much trust. If the AI does all the work, you learn less. Use AI to help you learn, not in place of your learning. Energy. Training and use of large models need much electricity and water for cooling. Caution Obey the rules of your school or company for the use of AI. Some rules tell you to say when AI helped you to make a document. Procedure: before you use AI-made content Do a check of all facts, numbers, names, and references in a reliable source. Make sure that the content does not copy the work of a different person. Make sure that the content does not contain private data. Read the content as your reader will read it. Remove errors and parts that are not clear. If your rules tell you to, say that AI helped to make the content. Accept responsibility for the final content. You are responsible for it, not the AI."},{"p":"generative-ai.html","m":5,"mt":"Generative AI","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"Generative AI makes new content: text, images, sound, video, and code. A chat AI is made in three stages: pretraining, fine-tuning, and human feedback. Temperature controls how predictable or varied the output is. The context window limits how much text a model can use at one time. An image generator starts from noise and removes it step by step. A good prompt gives the task, the context, the format, and an example. RAG lets a model use your documents and show its sources. AI-made content can be false. Always do a check before you use it."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of responsible AI. It tells you: The principles of responsible AI. How bias gets into an AI system, and how to find it. The main laws about personal data and AI. Why explanations and human checks are necessary. How to evaluate an AI tool before you trust it. When not to use AI. How to plan your own AI solution, step by step."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"what-responsible-ai-is","n":"2","t":"What responsible AI is","x":"An AI system can help many people. It can also cause harm. For example, it can refuse a loan to a person without a good reason, or it can show private data. Responsible AI is the work to prevent this harm. Most companies and governments use the same principles: Principle What it means Example question Fairness The system works equally well for all groups of people. Does the system make more errors for women, older people, or people from one region? Transparency People know that AI is used, and they know what it does. Do customers know that a chatbot is not a person? Privacy The system uses personal data only with permission, and it keeps the data safe. Do we need the name of the customer, or only the age? Safety The system does not cause physical or financial harm. What occurs when the system is wrong? Accountability A person or an organization is responsible for each decision. Who answers the complaint of a customer? Human oversight A person can check, change, or stop the decisions. Can a worker stop the system immediately? Note The AI does not have responsibility. People do. The organization that uses the system is responsible for its decisions."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"bias-and-fairness","n":"3","t":"Bias and fairness","x":"An AI system has bias when it makes more errors for some groups than for others. Bias can get into the system at each step of the ML workflow. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. Bias can get into an AI system at four steps. The red boxes show an example for each step. Real examples Face recognition. In 2018, the Gender Shades study tested face analysis systems from large companies. The systems made far more errors for women with dark skin than for men with light skin. The training data contained mostly photographs of men with light skin. Hiring. In 2018, news reports told that a large company stopped an AI hiring tool. The tool gave lower scores to CVs that contained the word \"women's\". It learned from ten years of old hiring decisions. Procedure: find bias Write down the groups that the system can treat differently, for example by gender, age, region, or language. Make sure that the test data has enough examples of each group. Measure the accuracy, precision, and recall for each group separately. Refer to Module 3, Section 10 . Compare the results. A large difference between groups is a sign of bias. If you find bias, collect more data for the weak groups, change the design, or do not use the system for these groups. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 6 of 8 Is the checker fair? Asha sells her mango checker to farmers in Gujarat. They grow Kesar mangoes. After one week, they complain: the checker puts many good Kesar mangoes into the damaged box. The team looks at the training data. 95% of the photographs show Alphonso mangoes from Asha's farm. Kesar mangoes have different natural marks on the skin. The model thinks that these marks are damage. The team does these steps: They measure the recall for each type of mango separately. They tell the farmers in Gujarat to stop using the checker until the problem is repaired. They collect 2,000 photographs of Kesar mangoes and train the model again. Now each new type of mango gets its own test before farmers use the checker. Think: why did the test in Part 3 not show this problem? The test set came from Asha's farm. It contained almost no Kesar mangoes. A test must contain examples of each group that will use the system. ← Part 5 Part 7 → Play video: Algorithmic Bias and Fairness (Crash Course AI) Video 1. Algorithmic bias and fairness (Crash Course AI) Open on YouTube Play video: How I'm fighting bias in algorithms (Joy Buolamwini, TED) Video 2. How I'm fighting bias in algorithms (Joy Buolamwini, TED) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"privacy-and-the-law","n":"4","t":"Privacy and the law","x":"Many AI systems use personal data . Personal data is any data that can identify a person, for example a name, a phone number, a photograph of a face, or a location. Laws about personal data India. The DPDP Act (Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023) tells organizations how to collect and use personal data. They must get permission for a clear purpose, keep the data safe, and let people correct or delete their data. European Union. The GDPR has been used since 2018. It also gives people rights about automatic decisions that have a large effect on them. Follow the principle of data minimization : collect only the data that the system really needs. Data that you do not have cannot be stolen. Laws about AI The European Union made the first large law about AI: the EU AI Act (2024). It puts AI systems into four levels of risk. A higher risk has stricter rules. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. The four levels of risk in the EU AI Act. Most AI systems are in the lowest level. Caution Laws are different in each country, and they change frequently. Before you use AI with personal data or for important decisions, get advice from a legal expert."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"explanations-and-human-oversight","n":"5","t":"Explanations and human oversight","x":"Module 4 tells you that it is difficult to know why a deep network gives an answer. But when a decision has a large effect on a person, the person must be able to ask \"why?\". Explainability is the ability to answer this question. Some methods help: Show the most important features. For example: \"The loan was refused mainly because of the low income and the short work history.\" Show similar examples. For example: \"Mangoes with these marks were damaged in 9 of 10 cases.\" Use a simpler model. For important decisions, a decision tree can be better than a deep network, because people can read it. Human oversight There are two common designs: Design How it works Use it when Human in the loop The AI suggests. A person approves each decision. Each error has a large effect, for example a medical diagnosis or a loan. Human on the loop The AI decides. A person monitors the results and can stop the system. There are many small decisions, for example spam filters or sorting mangoes. Warning A human check is useful only if the person has the time, the knowledge, and the authority to disagree with the AI. If a person always accepts the AI's suggestion, there is no real check."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"evaluate-an-ai-tool-before-you-trust-it","n":"6","t":"Evaluate an AI tool before you trust it","x":"Many companies sell AI tools. Their demonstrations show the best results. Do your own test with your own data. Procedure: evaluate an AI tool Write the task, and write what a good result is. Collect 20 to 50 real examples from your task. Include difficult examples. Test the tool on these examples. Count the errors, and look at each error. Compare the results for different groups of users. Read where the tool keeps your data, and if the company uses your data for training. Decide who checks the results, and who is responsible for errors. Start with a small, careful test. Monitor the results before you use the tool widely. Play video: 3 types of bias in AI (Google) Video 3. 3 types of bias in AI (Google) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"when-not-to-use-ai","n":"7","t":"When not to use AI","x":"AI is not always the best solution. A usual program, a simple rule, or a person can be better. Do not use AI in these cases: A simple rule works. If a person can write clear rules that are always correct, use a usual program. It is cheaper and easier to check. There is no good data. Without enough correct and fair examples, the model cannot learn. Nobody can check the errors. If an error can cause serious harm and no person can check the decisions, do not use AI. The law requires an explanation that you cannot give. The task needs human care. For example, telling a patient bad news. The cost is larger than the benefit. Include the cost of data, training, checks, and errors. Note \"Do not use AI\" is a correct result of a careful plan. It is not a failure."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"capstone-project-plan-an-ai-solution","n":"8","t":"Capstone project: plan an AI solution","x":"Now use all six modules. Select a problem from your work, your studies, or your town. Then answer the nine questions below. Each question has a link to the part of the manual that helps you. If you need an idea, select Load the mango example . It shows the plan for the mango checker. Project My AI plan Your answers are kept in this browser. You can download or print your plan at the end. The project worksheet needs JavaScript. Without JavaScript, write the answers to these questions on paper: the problem, if AI is the correct tool, the data, the type of model, the measure of success, the risks of bias, privacy, human oversight, and your decision."},{"p":"responsible-ai.html","m":6,"mt":"Responsible AI","id":"summary","n":"9","t":"Summary","x":"Responsible AI is fair, transparent, private, safe, and under human control. People are responsible, not the AI. Bias can get into a system through the data, the labels, the design, or the use. Measure the results for each group separately to find bias. Laws such as the DPDP Act, the GDPR, and the EU AI Act control personal data and AI. For important decisions, give explanations and let a qualified person check each decision. Test an AI tool with your own examples before you trust it. Sometimes the correct decision is not to use AI."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of the work that changes a model into a product. It tells you: The steps of the life of an AI system. How to prepare data, and how to prevent data leakage. Why each model needs a baseline. How to test a model fairly with cross-validation. How to put a model into use, and how to monitor it. How to build, test, and control an application that uses an LLM. How to use AI agents safely."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"from-a-model-to-a-product","n":"2","t":"From a model to a product","x":"In a project notebook, a model is finished when its test result is good. In a product, that is only the start. Real users send new data each day. The world changes. The model must stay correct, fast, safe, and affordable. The life of an AI system is a loop, not a line. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. The life of an AI system. After deployment, monitoring finds new problems, and the loop starts again. Note In 2015, Google engineers wrote that in real ML systems, the model code is only a small part of all the code. The data pipelines, tests, monitoring, and settings around it are much larger. Thus plan time for all six steps, not only for training. Play video: A Chat with Andrew on MLOps: From Model-centric to Data-centric AI (DeepLearningAI) Video 1. From model-centric to data-centric AI (Andrew Ng, DeepLearning.AI) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"prepare-the-data","n":"3","t":"Prepare the data","x":"Real data is never clean. Before training, do these checks. Refer also to Module 3, Section 4 . Missing values Some examples do not have a value for a feature. For example, the camera did not measure the weight of some mangoes. You can: Remove the examples with missing values, if there are only a few. Put the median of the feature in the empty places. We call this imputation . Add a feature \"value is missing\" (yes or no). Sometimes the fact that a value is missing is useful information. Categories A model can use only numbers. A feature such as \"type of mango\" contains words. One-hot encoding changes each category into its own column with 0 or 1: Type of mango Alphonso Kesar Totapuri Alphonso 1 0 0 Kesar 0 1 0 Totapuri 0 0 1 Do not change categories into 1, 2, and 3. The model would think that Totapuri is \"more\" than Alphonso. Other checks Duplicates. Remove examples that occur two times. Units. Make sure that all values use the same unit, for example grams and not grams and kilograms together. Scale. Standardize features that have very different sizes. Refer to Module 2, Section 5 . Data leakage Data leakage occurs when the model learns from information that it will not have in real use. The test result is excellent, but the model fails with real users. Warning Examples of data leakage: (1) A feature \"customer complained\" helps to predict damaged mangoes, but a complaint occurs only after the sale. (2) The same mango is photographed two times, and one photograph goes into the training set and one into the test set. (3) You calculate the mean for standardization with all the data, before you divide it. Always divide the data first. Then calculate everything from the training set only. Procedure: check for data leakage For each feature, ask: \"Will this value be available at the time of the prediction?\" If not, remove it. Make sure that copies of the same item cannot go into two different sets. Divide by item, by customer, or by date. Divide the data before you clean, scale, or select features. If a result looks too good, for example 100% accuracy, look for leakage first."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"start-with-a-baseline","n":"4","t":"Start with a baseline","x":"A baseline is a very simple method that you compare your model with. If your model is not better than the baseline, it is not useful. Task A usual baseline Classification Always predict the most frequent category. Regression Always predict the mean of the training labels. Forecast for tomorrow Predict the same value as today. Any task A simple rule that an expert writes, or the result of a person. Example: in the spam data of Project 3 , 4,825 of 5,572 messages are not spam. A \"model\" that always says \"not spam\" is 86.6% accurate. Thus a real model with 90% accuracy is only a small improvement. The model in Project 3 has 99%, so it is much better than the baseline. But also remember: the baseline finds zero spam. Its recall is 0%. Note Start each project with the simplest model that can work. Use a complex model only if it is clearly better. A simple model is cheaper, faster, and easier to explain."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"test-fairly-with-cross-validation","n":"5","t":"Test fairly with cross-validation","x":"With one division of the data, the result depends on luck: some test sets are easier than others. Cross-validation gives a more reliable result. Refer to Figure 2. Divide the training data into 5 equal parts. We call each part a fold. Train the model on 4 parts. Test it on the fifth part. Do this 5 times. Each time, use a different part for the test. Calculate the mean and the spread of the 5 results. Figure 2. Five-fold cross-validation. Each part is the test part one time. The final test set stays separate. Use it only one time, at the end. Hyperparameters Training finds the weights. But a person selects some settings before training, for example the learning rate, the number of layers, or the number of groups in k-means. We call these settings hyperparameters . To select them, try different values, and compare the cross-validation results. Never use the final test set for this. Refer to Module 3, Section 4 . Play video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: Cross Validation (StatQuest) Video 2. Machine learning fundamentals: cross validation (StatQuest) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"put-a-model-into-use","n":"6","t":"Put a model into use","x":"Deployment makes the model available to its users. There are three usual ways: Way How it works Good for Batch The model makes many predictions at one time, for example each night. Reports, recommendations, risk lists. Online (server) An app sends one request. The model answers in less than one second. Chat AI, search, fraud checks during a payment. On the device The model runs on a phone, a camera, or a machine. Work without internet, privacy, very fast answers. The mango checker at the sorting table. Important measurements in use Latency : the time for one answer. A sorting machine needs an answer in milliseconds. Cost: the price of computers or of an AI service for each prediction. Availability: what occurs when the model or the internet does not work. Always have a fallback, for example a person or a simple rule. Procedure: release a new model safely Give each model a version number. Keep the old version. Run the new model in shadow mode first: it makes predictions on real data, but nobody uses them. Compare them with the current system. Give the new model to a small part of the users, for example 5%. Compare the results of the two groups. We call this an A/B test. If the results are good, give the new model to all users. If a problem occurs, go back to the old version immediately."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"monitor-the-model","n":"7","t":"Monitor the model","x":"A model does not change after training. But the world changes. Then the model becomes less accurate, often without an error message. There are two types of change: Data drift : the inputs change. Example: a new type of mango, a new camera, or summer light in place of winter light. Concept drift : the meaning of the correct answer changes. Example: spam senders change their words, so old spam words do not work any more. What to monitor The results that you can check: a sample of predictions each week, checked by a person. The inputs: are the means and the distributions still similar to the training data? The outputs: did the percentage of \"damaged\" predictions suddenly change? Complaints, latency, and cost. Set a limit for each measurement. If a value goes past the limit, the system sends an alert, and a person examines the problem. Often the solution is to collect new data and train the model again. To build this monitoring loop in code, do Project 8 . Try it Keep a model accurate for 18 months After month 6, a new type of mango comes to the farm, and the recall of the checker starts to decrease. Turn monitoring on or off, and change the alert limit. Find a good balance between the recall and the cost of training again. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 7 of 8 One year later The mango checker now works on 12 farms. One year after the first release, the team looks at its monitoring dashboard: Each week, a worker checks 200 random mangoes from each box. The recall for damaged mangoes was 97%. In the last month, it decreased to 88%. The cause: a new camera on three farms. Its pictures are brighter. This is data drift. The team collects 1,500 photographs from the new cameras, trains the model again, and tests it in shadow mode for one week. Then they release version 2.1. The recall is 96% again. The farmers' chatbot from Part 5 now answers 3,000 questions each day. The team tests each new prompt on a set of 50 questions before they change it. Think: why did the team find the problem only through the weekly check, and not through an error message? The model did not stop. It continued to give answers, but more of them were wrong. A model that gets worse looks the same as a good model. Only a check of real results shows the problem. ← Part 6 Part 8 →"},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"build-with-large-language-models","n":"8","t":"Build with large language models","x":"Many AI products today do not train a model. They use an LLM from a company, and add a prompt, documents (RAG), and tools. Refer to Module 5, Section 8 . This is fast, but it needs its own tests and controls. Test an LLM application An LLM gives different answers each time, and a small change in the prompt can change many answers. Thus make an evaluation set : a fixed list of example questions with good answers. Use it like the test in Project 6 . Collect 50 to 200 real questions, including difficult ones and questions that the system must refuse. For each question, write what a good answer must contain. Each time you change the prompt, the documents, or the model, run all the questions again. Compare the score with the previous version. Do not release a version with a lower score. Some teams use a second LLM to grade the answers. This saves time, but check a sample of its grades by hand. The grader can also make errors. Guardrails Guardrails are checks around the model: Input checks: remove private data, and stop requests that are not allowed. Output checks: check the format, check that the answer uses the documents, and stop harmful content. Defense against prompt injection: treat text from web pages and documents as data, not as instructions. Refer to Module 5, Section 9 . Calculate the cost AI services usually charge for each token, and output tokens cost more than input tokens. Example, with prices that are only for this calculation: input costs ₹250 for 1 million tokens, and output costs ₹1,000 for 1 million tokens. one answer = 2,000 input tokens × ₹250 ÷ 1,000,000 + 300 output tokens × ₹1,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = ₹0.50 + ₹0.30 = ₹0.80 For 3,000 answers each day, the cost is approximately ₹2,400 each day. RAG adds input tokens, because the found text goes into each prompt. Thus send only the parts of the documents that are necessary. Play video: State of GPT (Andrej Karpathy, Microsoft Build) Video 3. State of GPT (Andrej Karpathy, Microsoft Build) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"agents-in-practice","n":"9","t":"Agents in practice","x":"An AI agent works in a loop. It plans a step, uses a tool, looks at the result, and plans the next step. Refer to Figure 3. Figure 3. The loop of an AI agent. Important actions wait for a person. The loop stops at a maximum number of steps. Workflow or agent? If the steps are always the same, use a fixed workflow: step 1, then step 2, then step 3. It is cheaper, faster, and easier to test. Use an agent only when the steps must change for each task. Rules for safe agents Give the agent only the tools and the permissions that it needs. For example, it can read the calendar, but it cannot delete events. Let a person approve each important action. Refer to Module 6, Section 5 . Set a maximum number of steps and a maximum cost for each task. Keep a record of each step, so that you can find the cause of an error. Test the agent with an evaluation set, as for each LLM application."},{"p":"ai-in-practice.html","m":7,"mt":"AI in practice","id":"summary","n":"10","t":"Summary","x":"The model is only one step in the life of an AI system. Plan for data, evaluation, deployment, and monitoring. Clean the data, and look for data leakage. Divide the data before you prepare it. Compare each model with a simple baseline. Cross-validation gives a more reliable result than one test. Select hyperparameters with it, not with the final test set. Release new models step by step: shadow mode, a small A/B test, then all users. Keep the old version. Monitor real results. Data drift and concept drift make a model worse without an error message. Test LLM applications with an evaluation set, use guardrails, and calculate the cost. Use agents only when necessary, with limited tools, limits, records, and human approval."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This module gives a description of the engineering work that makes an AI product reliable. It tells you: What DevOps is, and what MLOps and LLMOps add for AI. How to keep versions of code, data, models, and prompts. How to serve a model as an API, and how to put it in a container. How automatic tests and a CI/CD pipeline release changes safely. How to run the service in the cloud, and how to observe it. How to keep secrets and the system safe. Note The code in this module is tested with Python, scikit-learn 1.9, and FastAPI. To run it, you need Python on your computer. Docker is necessary only for Section 6 ."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"what-devops-is","n":"2","t":"What DevOps is","x":"In the past, one team wrote the software (development) and a different team ran it on the servers (operations). The two teams frequently blamed each other for problems, and releases were slow and risky. DevOps joins the two types of work. Small changes, frequently. A small change is easy to test, and easy to remove if it is wrong. Automation. Machines do the tests, the builds, and the releases. People do not do the same steps by hand each time. Fast feedback. The team sees immediately if a change causes a problem, in the tests or in production. Shared responsibility. The team that builds the service also helps to operate it. The work is a loop with no end. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. The DevOps loop. The left side is development, the right side is operations. The work goes around again and again, in small steps. Teams frequently measure DevOps with four numbers: how frequently they release, how long a change takes to reach users, how many releases cause a problem, and how long it takes to repair a problem. Play video: What is DevOps? REALLY understand it (TechWorld with Nana) Video 1. What is DevOps? (TechWorld with Nana) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"mlops-and-llmops","n":"3","t":"MLOps and LLMOps: what is different for AI","x":"Usual software changes only when a person changes the code. An AI system can also change when the data changes, and it can become worse without a change in the code. Refer to Module 7, Section 7 . Thus AI teams add more work to DevOps. Practice What it adds DevOps Versions of code, automatic tests, automatic releases, monitoring of the servers. MLOps Versions of data and models, automatic model tests, a model registry, monitoring for drift, and automatic training again. LLMOps Versions of prompts, evaluation sets that run in the pipeline, guardrails, and monitoring of token cost and answer quality. A team does not need all of this on the first day. Start with the basics, and add more when the product grows: Level 0: a person trains the model in a notebook and copies the file to a server by hand. Level 1: the code is in Git, the tests are automatic, and a pipeline releases the service. Level 2: the pipeline also trains and tests new models, and the monitoring starts training again when drift occurs."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"version-everything","n":"4","t":"Keep a version of everything","x":"If a problem occurs, you must know exactly what changed, and you must be able to go back. Thus keep a version of each part of the system. Part Usual tool Code and settings Git , with a shared copy on GitHub or GitLab. Data A data versioning tool, for example DVC, or dated copies in cloud storage that nobody changes. Models A model registry : each model with its version, its training data, and its test results. Prompts In Git, as files, the same as code. Libraries A requirements file with exact version numbers. Procedure: make a change with Git Make a new branch for the change, for example git switch -c new-threshold . Make the change. Save it in small steps with git commit . Write a short message that tells why. Send the branch to GitHub, and open a pull request . The automatic tests run. Another person reviews the change. When the tests pass and the review is good, join the branch into main . The pipeline then releases it. Play video: Git Explained in 100 Seconds (Fireship) Video 2. Git explained in 100 seconds (Fireship) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"serve-the-model-as-an-api","n":"5","t":"Serve the model as an API","x":"An app, a website, or a machine on the sorting line must ask the model for predictions. The usual way is an API : the program sends a request with the features, and the service sends back the prediction. Refer to Module 7, Section 6 . Step 1: train and save the model This program makes example data for 2,000 mangoes, trains a model, and saves it to a file with a version number. train.py Copy the code import json import joblib import numpy as np from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier from sklearn.metrics import recall_score from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split # 1. Make example data: 2,000 mangoes with three features each. rng = np.random.default_rng(0) n = 2000 weight = rng.normal(250, 40, n) # grams firmness = rng.normal(6, 1.5, n) # 0 (soft) to 10 (hard) spots = rng.poisson(1.5, n) # dark spots on the skin damaged = ((spots >= 4) | (firmness < 3.5)).astype(int) wrong = rng.random(n) < 0.01 # 1% of the labels are wrong, as in real data damaged = damaged ^ wrong X = np.column_stack([weight, firmness, spots]) # 2. Divide the data, train the model, and measure it. X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split( X, damaged, test_size=0.2, random_state=1, stratify=damaged) model = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=1) model.fit(X_train, y_train) recall = recall_score(y_test, model.predict(X_test)) print(\"Recall for damaged mangoes:\", round(recall, 3)) # 3. Save the model and its results, with a version number. joblib.dump(model, \"model.joblib\") with open(\"model_info.json\", \"w\") as f: json.dump({\"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"recall\": round(recall, 3)}, f) Step 2: make the web service This program uses FastAPI. It loads the model one time when it starts. It has two addresses: /health tells if the service works, and /predict gives a prediction. The Mango class checks each input. A weight of −5 grams is refused. app.py Copy the code import json import joblib from fastapi import FastAPI from pydantic import BaseModel, Field app = FastAPI(title=\"Mango checker\") model = joblib.load(\"model.joblib\") with open(\"model_info.json\") as f: info = json.load(f) # The input that the API accepts. Values outside these limits are refused. class Mango(BaseModel): weight_g: float = Field(gt=0, lt=1000) firmness: float = Field(ge=0, le=10) spots: int = Field(ge=0, le=100) @app.get(\"/health\") def health(): return {\"status\": \"ok\", \"model_version\": info[\"version\"]} @app.post(\"/predict\") def predict(mango: Mango): p = model.predict_proba([[mango.weight_g, mango.firmness, mango.spots]])[0][1] return {\"damaged\": bool(p >= 0.5), \"probability\": round(float(p), 3), \"model_version\": info[\"version\"]} Step 3: run it Put the two files and requirements.txt (refer to Section 6 ) in one folder. Then type these commands in a terminal: Terminal Copy the commands pip install -r requirements.txt python train.py uvicorn app:app --port 8000 The training prints Recall for damaged mangoes: 0.98 . Then open http://localhost:8000/docs in a browser. FastAPI makes a page where you can send test requests. A request with {\"weight_g\": 240, \"firmness\": 2, \"spots\": 6} gives {\"damaged\": true, \"probability\": 1.0, \"model_version\": \"1.0.0\"} . Note Each answer contains the model version. When a user reports a wrong answer, the team knows immediately which model gave it."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"package-it-in-a-container","n":"6","t":"Package it in a container","x":"\"It works on my computer\" is a usual problem. The server has a different version of Python or of a library, and the service fails. Two solutions work together: Exact versions. A requirements file gives the exact version of each library. A container . It packs the program with everything that it needs. The same container runs the same way on your computer, on the test server, and in production. requirements.txt Copy fastapi==0.135.1 uvicorn==0.41.0 scikit-learn==1.9.0 numpy==2.4.6 joblib==1.5.3 httpx==0.28.1 pytest==9.1.1 A Dockerfile gives the steps to build the container image: Dockerfile Copy FROM python:3.12-slim WORKDIR /app COPY requirements.txt . RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt COPY app.py model.joblib model_info.json ./ EXPOSE 8000 CMD [\"uvicorn\", \"app:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"] Build the image with a version tag, and start it: Terminal Copy the commands docker build -t mango-checker:1.0.0 . docker run -p 8000:8000 mango-checker:1.0.0 Environments Teams usually have three copies of the system. The same container image goes through all three: Development: on the computer of the developer. Staging: a copy of production, for the last tests. It does not have real users. Production: the system that real users use. Play video: Docker in 100 Seconds (Fireship) Video 3. Docker in 100 seconds (Fireship) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"test-and-release-automatically","n":"7","t":"Test and release automatically","x":"CI/CD means continuous integration and continuous delivery. Each change goes through the same automatic steps, called a pipeline . Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. A CI/CD pipeline. Each change goes through the same checks. A change reaches all users only if it passes all of them. Tests for an AI service Test more than the code. These tests check the API, the input checks, and the quality of the model: test_app.py Copy the code from fastapi.testclient import TestClient from app import app, info client = TestClient(app) def test_health(): assert client.get(\"/health\").status_code == 200 def test_good_mango(): r = client.post(\"/predict\", json={\"weight_g\": 260, \"firmness\": 7, \"spots\": 0}) assert r.json()[\"damaged\"] is False def test_damaged_mango(): r = client.post(\"/predict\", json={\"weight_g\": 240, \"firmness\": 2, \"spots\": 6}) assert r.json()[\"damaged\"] is True def test_bad_input_is_refused(): r = client.post(\"/predict\", json={\"weight_g\": -5, \"firmness\": 7, \"spots\": 0}) assert r.status_code == 422 def test_model_is_good_enough(): assert info[\"recall\"] >= 0.90 Run them with the command pytest . The last test is a quality gate : if a new model has a recall of less than 90%, the test fails, and the pipeline does not release that model. The pipeline file GitHub Actions runs this pipeline automatically for each pull request and for each change to main . Put the file in the folder .github/workflows . .github/workflows/ci.yml Copy name: Test and build on: push: branches: [main] pull_request: jobs: test-and-build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-python@v5 with: python-version: \"3.12\" - run: pip install -r requirements.txt - run: python train.py - run: pytest - run: docker build -t mango-checker:${{ github.sha }} . For an LLM application, add the evaluation set to the pipeline. Refer to Module 7, Section 8 . A change to a prompt is then tested the same as a change to code. To set up this pipeline on GitHub step by step, do Project 9 . Try it Stop bad changes before they reach users Eight changes go through your pipeline this month. Five of them contain a problem. Select the checks in the pipeline, and look at which problems reach the users. Each check takes time for each change. The canary release gives each change to 5% of the users for one hour. This activity needs JavaScript. Turn on JavaScript in your browser to use it. Play video: DevOps CI/CD Explained in 100 Seconds (Fireship) Video 4. DevOps CI/CD explained in 100 seconds (Fireship) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"run-it-in-the-cloud","n":"8","t":"Run it in the cloud","x":"Most teams rent servers from a cloud company in place of buying them. There are different levels of service. Refer to the table. Option You manage Good for Virtual machine The operating system, the container, the updates. Full control, special hardware. Container service Only the container. The cloud starts and stops copies. Most web services and APIs. Kubernetes A cluster of many containers and services. Large systems with many services and a platform team. Serverless functions Only the code of one function. Small tasks that run sometimes. Not for large models. Managed AI service Only the prompts and the data. The company runs the model. LLM applications. Refer to Module 5 . Scale and cost Autoscaling : the cloud starts more copies of the container when there are many requests, and stops them when there are few. A load balancer divides the requests between the copies. GPUs are expensive. Use them only for models that need them. A small model like the mango checker runs well on a usual processor. Cost alerts: set a monthly budget and an alert. A mistake in autoscaling can cost much money in one night. Infrastructure as code Do not make the servers, the networks, and the databases by hand in the web console of the cloud. Write them in files, for example with Terraform. We call this infrastructure as code . The files go into Git, so a change to the servers gets a review, and you can make the same system again after a disaster. Play video: Kubernetes Explained in 100 Seconds (Fireship) Video 5. Kubernetes explained in 100 seconds (Fireship) Open on YouTube"},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"observe-the-system-in-production","n":"9","t":"Observe the system in production","x":"Module 7 tells you how to monitor the quality of the model. The service around the model also needs monitoring. We call the ability to see what occurs inside a running system observability . It uses three types of data. Refer to Figure 3. Figure 3. The three types of observability data. People look at dashboards. Alerts call a person when a limit is passed. Set clear targets A service level objective (SLO) is a target that the team agrees on. Examples: Availability: 99.9% of the requests are successful each month. In a month of 30 days, this allows 30 × 24 × 60 × 0.001 = 43.2 minutes without service. Latency: 95% of the answers come in less than 100 ms. We call this the p95 latency. Use the p95, not the mean. The mean hides the slow answers that make users angry. Procedure: when an alert comes Confirm the problem on the dashboard. Tell the team that you are working on it. Stop the damage first. If a new release caused the problem, do a rollback to the previous version. Find the cause with the logs and the traces. Repair the cause. Release the repair through the normal pipeline. Write a short report without blame: what occurred, why, and what change prevents it next time. We call this a postmortem. 🥭 Project: the mango checker · Part 8 of 8 Forty farms, one service The mango checker is now a web service in the cloud. Forty farms send it 2 million requests each day. The team uses a pipeline for each change. The team releases model version 2.2. All tests pass. In the canary release, the dashboard shows that the p95 latency increases from 40 ms to 600 ms. The sorting lines on two farms become slow. The person on call does a rollback to version 2.1 in two minutes. Only the farms in the canary group were affected, for 20 minutes. The postmortem finds the cause: version 2.2 is a much larger model. The team adds a load test to the pipeline. Now a model that is too slow cannot pass the pipeline. Think: all the tests passed. Why did the problem still occur, and why was the damage small? The tests checked the accuracy, but not the speed. The canary release showed the problem with real traffic, but only to 5% of the users. The fast rollback stopped the damage. Each layer of protection catches some problems that the other layers miss. ← Part 7 This is the last part of the project."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"security-and-secrets","n":"10","t":"Security and secrets","x":"A secret is a password, an API key, or a token. Many security problems start with a secret in the wrong place. Warning Never put a secret in the code or in Git. Automatic programs search public repositories for keys all the time, and they can use a key minutes after it is published. If a secret gets into Git, change the secret immediately. To remove it from the code is not sufficient, because the history of Git still contains it. Keep secrets in a secret manager or in the secret settings of the pipeline. The program reads them from environment variables when it starts. Give the least access. Each service gets only the permissions that it needs. Refer to Module 7, Section 9 . Scan automatically. Add a secret scan and a check of the libraries for known security problems to the pipeline. Check all inputs. The API refuses values that are not possible, as the Mango class does. For LLM applications, also defend against prompt injection. Refer to Module 5, Section 9 . Protect personal data in logs too. Do not write names, phone numbers, or full prompts with private data into the logs. Refer to Module 6, Section 4 ."},{"p":"devops.html","m":8,"mt":"DevOps for AI","id":"summary","n":"11","t":"Summary","x":"DevOps joins development and operations: small changes, automation, fast feedback, and shared responsibility. MLOps adds versions of data and models, model tests, and training again. LLMOps adds prompt versions, evaluation sets, and cost monitoring. Keep a version of code, data, models, prompts, and libraries. Make changes on a branch, with a pull request and a review. Serve the model as an API. Check each input, and send the model version with each answer. Pin the library versions, and put the service in a container. The same image goes to development, staging, and production. A CI/CD pipeline tests and releases each change. Quality gates stop bad models. A canary release tests with real traffic. Use logs, metrics, and traces. Set SLOs. When a problem occurs, roll back first, then find the cause. Never put secrets in code. Give the least access, and scan the code automatically."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"Computer vision is the part of AI that gets information from images and video. This topic gives a description of the four most usual tasks, how object detection works, where people use computer vision, and its limits."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"four-tasks","n":"2","t":"Four tasks","x":"The same photograph can give very different types of answer. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. One photograph, four tasks. Classification only says what is in the image. The other tasks also say where. Task Output Example question Classification One label (and a probability) for the full image. Is this mango damaged? Object detection A box, a label, and a confidence for each object. How many mangoes are on this tree? Segmentation A label for each pixel. How much of the leaf has the disease? OCR (text recognition) The text in the image. What is written on the box? A fifth task finds key points , for example the elbows, knees, and shoulders of a person. Fitness apps and sports analysis use key points to measure the movements of the body."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"how-object-detection-works","n":"3","t":"How object detection works","x":"A detection model is usually a CNN or a vision transformer . Refer to Module 4, Section 6 . For each part of the image, it predicts: The four numbers of a bounding box : the position and the size of a rectangle. A probability for each category, for example mango, leaf, or nothing. The model finds many boxes that are almost the same for one mango. A step with the name non-maximum suppression keeps the box with the highest confidence and removes the other boxes that overlap it. Is a box correct? To measure a detection model, compare each predicted box with the correct box. IoU (intersection over union) measures how well two boxes overlap. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. Intersection over union (IoU). The orange area is the overlap. Example: each box has an area of 100 cm² on the screen, and the overlap is 60 cm². The two boxes together cover 100 + 100 − 60 = 140 cm². IoU = 60 ÷ 140 = 0.43. This is less than 0.5, thus the detection is not correct. When you know which detections are correct, you can calculate precision and recall, as in Module 3, Section 10 ."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"segmentation-and-video","n":"4","t":"Segmentation and video","x":"Semantic segmentation gives each pixel a category, for example \"mango\" or \"leaf\". Two mangoes that touch become one area. Instance segmentation also separates the objects: \"mango 1\" and \"mango 2\". Figure 1C shows this. Tracking connects the detections in each frame of a video, so that the system knows that it is the same mango or the same car. Most teams do not train a vision model from zero. They start with a model that is already trained on millions of images and train it a little more on their own images. This is transfer learning. Refer to Module 4, Section 9 . New multimodal models can also describe an image in words and answer questions about it. Refer to Module 5, Section 3 . Note Labels for detection and segmentation take much more work than one label for each image. A person must draw each box or each object outline. Plan time and money for this work."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"where-people-use-computer-vision","n":"5","t":"Where people use computer vision","x":"Area Task Example Agriculture Detection, segmentation Count fruit on trees. Find diseased leaves. Measure the size of fruit on a sorting line. Medicine Segmentation, classification Mark possible tumors on scans, for a doctor to check. Factories Detection, classification Find scratches and defects on parts. Transport Detection, tracking Driver assistance finds people, cars, and road signs. Read number plates. Shops and stores Detection Find empty shelves. Count stock in a store room. Offices OCR Read invoices, forms, and identity documents. Phones Classification, key points Unlock the phone with a face. Find photographs by their content."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"limits-and-risks","n":"6","t":"Limits and risks","x":"New conditions. A new camera, different light, rain, or a new type of object can decrease the accuracy. This is data drift. Refer to Module 7, Section 7 . Small and hidden objects. Objects that are small, far away, or partly hidden are difficult to find. Attacks. A special printed pattern can make a model see something that is not there, or not see something that is there. Bias. A face model that is trained mostly on one group of people can make more errors for other groups. Refer to Module 6, Section 3 . Warning A photograph of a face is personal data. Face recognition in public places has strict rules in many countries, for example in the EU AI Act. Refer to Module 6, Section 4 . Do not use face recognition without a legal check."},{"p":"topic-computer-vision.html","w":"Topic: Computer vision","id":"summary","n":"7","t":"Summary","x":"Classification says what is in an image. Detection, segmentation, and OCR also say where. A detection model predicts a box, a label, and a confidence for each object. IoU measures how well a predicted box matches the correct box. 0.5 or more is a usual limit. Instance segmentation separates the objects. Tracking follows them through a video. Use transfer learning, and plan for the work of drawing the labels. Monitor for new conditions, and take special care with faces."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"Natural language processing (NLP) is the part of AI that works with human language. This topic gives a description of the types of language task, how a computer finds names in text, how search by meaning works, and how to select a model."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"types-of-language-task","n":"2","t":"Types of language task","x":"Most language tasks have one of four shapes. The shape is the type of output. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. Four shapes of language task. The input is always text. The output is a label, a label for each word, new text, or an answer. Shape Tasks 1. One label Sentiment analysis , spam filters, sorting of support emails by subject, detection of harmful text. 2. A label for each word Named entity recognition (NER), the parts of speech of each word. 3. New text Machine translation, summaries, correction of grammar, text from speech. 4. An answer Questions and answers with RAG. Refer to Module 5, Section 8 ."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"how-a-computer-finds-names-in-text","n":"3","t":"How a computer finds names in text","x":"NER is a classification for each token, not for the full text. The model reads all the text with attention, so it knows the context of each word. Then it gives each token a label. Refer to Module 4, Section 8 . Token Asha sent 40 boxes to Pune on Monday Label PERSON – AMOUNT – – PLACE – DATE Figure 2. NER gives each token a label. A dash means \"not a name\". The context is important. In \"Pune is the city where I live\", Pune is a place. In \"The Pune team won\", Pune is part of the name of an organization. A model that only looks at one word at a time cannot see this difference. Companies use NER to get data from documents automatically: the names, the dates, and the amounts in invoices, contracts, or medical notes."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"search-by-meaning","n":"4","t":"Search by meaning","x":"A usual search finds documents that contain the same words as the question. Semantic search finds documents with the same meaning, also when the words are different. A model changes each document into an embedding: a list of numbers that keeps its meaning. Refer to Module 4, Section 7 . The model changes the question into an embedding in the same way. The system calculates the cosine similarity between the question and each document. Refer to Module 2, Section 9 . The system shows the documents with the highest similarity first. Question: \"My mango has black spots\" Same words? Similarity (example) \"Anthracnose: a fungus disease that makes dark marks on the fruit skin\" No 0.82 \"How to keep mangoes fresh at home\" Yes: \"mango\" 0.41 \"Prices of black pepper this week\" Yes: \"black\" 0.12 The best answer does not contain any word of the question. A word search would show it last. RAG uses semantic search to find the parts of documents that go into the prompt. Speech Speech recognition changes speech into text. Then the system can use all the text tasks on it. Voice assistants do this: speech to text, then a language task, then text to speech for the answer."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"a-small-model-or-a-large-language-model","n":"5","t":"A small model or a large language model?","x":"You can do most text tasks in two ways. Refer to the table. Point Small special model Large language model with a prompt Training You fine-tune it with hundreds or thousands of labeled examples. No training. You write a prompt with some examples. Speed and cost Very fast and cheap. It can run on your own computer. Slower. You usually pay for each token. Flexibility It does one task only. It can do many tasks, and new tasks immediately. Output Always in the same format. Can change. Needs output checks. Good for Millions of texts each day, with a fixed task. A new task, few examples, or a task that changes. Note A usual plan: start with an LLM and a prompt, to test the idea quickly. Use its answers, checked by people, as labels. If the volume becomes large, train a small model with these labels. Always compare the two with the same evaluation set. Refer to Module 7, Section 8 ."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"where-people-use-language-ai","n":"6","t":"Where people use language AI","x":"Area Example Customer support Sort incoming messages by subject and urgency. Suggest answers to the support team. Business Find the opinions in thousands of product reviews. Get data from invoices and contracts. Government and health Translate public information into many languages. Make notes from a doctor's conversation, for the doctor to check. Search Search by meaning in company documents, help pages, and laws. Accessibility Subtitles for videos, voice control for people who cannot use a keyboard."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"limits-and-risks","n":"7","t":"Limits and risks","x":"Languages with less data. Models are usually best in English. Many Indian languages have less training text, so the results can be worse. Text that mixes languages, for example Hindi and English in one sentence, is also more difficult. Test with your own users' language. False content. A summary or a translation can add, remove, or change facts. Check important texts. Bias. A sentiment model can give lower scores to some dialects or names. Measure the results for each group. Refer to Module 6, Section 3 . Private data. Emails, chats, and medical notes contain personal data. Remove it or get permission before you use it."},{"p":"topic-language-tasks.html","w":"Topic: Language tasks","id":"summary","n":"8","t":"Summary","x":"Language tasks give one label, a label for each word, new text, or an answer from documents. NER gives each token a label. It needs the context of the full sentence. Semantic search compares embeddings. It finds the same meaning with different words. A small special model is fast and cheap for one fixed task. An LLM is flexible and needs no training. Test with the languages of your users, and check important outputs."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"A recommendation system predicts which items a person will probably like, and shows those items first. An item can be a product, a video, a song, a job, or a news article. This topic gives a description of the two main methods, the stages of a large system, how to measure it, and its risks."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"two-main-methods","n":"2","t":"Two main methods","x":"Method The idea It needs Content-based filtering \"This is similar to what you liked.\" Information about the items, for example the type, the price, or the words in the description. Collaborative filtering \"People like you also liked this.\" The history of many users: what they bought, watched, or rated. Collaborative filtering starts from a table of users and items. Most cells are empty, because each person used only a few items. The system must predict the empty cells. Refer to Figure 1. Figure 1. A table of users and items. The system predicts the empty cells. Sara gives ratings like Ravi, so she will probably not like the pickle. All values are only examples."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"how-the-prediction-works","n":"3","t":"How the prediction works","x":"Modern systems learn an embedding for each user and for each item. Refer to Module 4, Section 7 . The numbers are learned from the history. Often they agree with ideas that people can name, for example \"sweet\" and \"sour\". The predicted score is the dot product of the user embedding and the item embedding. Refer to Module 2, Section 9 . Example with two numbers (sweet, sour): Embedding Sweet Sour Score for Sara (0.9, 0.1) Alphonso 0.8 0.2 0.9 × 0.8 + 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.74 Kesar 0.7 0.3 0.9 × 0.7 + 0.1 × 0.3 = 0.66 Raw mango pickle 0.1 0.9 0.9 × 0.1 + 0.1 × 0.9 = 0.18 The system shows the items with the highest scores first: Alphonso, then Kesar. Training changes the embeddings with gradient descent, so that the scores agree with the real history. Real systems use 32 to several hundred numbers for each embedding, not two. The cold start problem A new user or a new item has no history. We call this the cold start problem. Solutions: Show popular items to a new user, or ask the user to select some interests. Use content information for a new item, for example its type and price."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"the-stages-of-a-large-system","n":"4","t":"The stages of a large system","x":"A video app can have millions of videos. It cannot calculate a full score for each video, for each user, each time. Thus a large system works in stages. Refer to Figure 2. Figure 2. The stages of a large recommendation system. Each stage uses a more detailed model on fewer items. Candidate generation . A fast method selects approximately 1,000 possible items, for example with a search for the nearest embeddings. Ranking. A more detailed model predicts the probability that the user will click, buy, or watch to the end. It uses many features, for example the time of day and the device. Rules. The system removes items that are not suitable and adds variety. Each action of the user goes back into the data. Thus the system learns all the time."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"how-to-measure-recommendations","n":"5","t":"How to measure recommendations","x":"Before release: hide the last purchases of each user. Then check if the system recommends them. Precision at k gives the part of the first k items that the user really used. Example: 3 of the first 10 items were bought, so precision at 10 = 0.3. After release: use an A/B test. Refer to Module 7, Section 6 . Compare clicks, purchases, and returns. Long term: do users stay satisfied after months? A system that gets many clicks today can make users leave later. Caution Divide the history by date, not at random. Train with the older history, and test with the newer history. Otherwise the model sees the future during training. This is data leakage. Refer to Module 7, Section 3 ."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"where-people-use-recommendation-systems","n":"6","t":"Where people use recommendation systems","x":"Area Item What the system usually tries to increase Online shops Products Purchases, and the value of each order. Video and music apps Videos, songs Watch time or listen time, and return visits. News and social media Articles, posts Clicks, comments, and time in the app. Jobs Job offers, candidates Applications and successful hires. Food delivery and travel Restaurants, hotels Orders and bookings."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"risks-of-recommendation-systems","n":"7","t":"Risks of recommendation systems","x":"Feedback loops . The system shows popular items, so they get more clicks, so the system shows them more. New items and small sellers get few chances. Filter bubbles. A person sees more and more of the same opinions or the same type of content. The wrong goal. A system that only increases watch time can recommend content that is extreme or harmful, because it holds attention. Fairness. A job system that learns from old hiring decisions can repeat old bias. Refer to Module 6, Section 3 . Privacy. The history of a person shows much about their life. Protect it, and tell users what data the system uses. Good teams add variety, measure long-term satisfaction, and give users control, for example \"Not interested\" and \"Show fewer items like this\"."},{"p":"topic-recommendation-systems.html","w":"Topic: Recommendation systems","id":"summary","n":"8","t":"Summary","x":"Content-based filtering recommends similar items. Collaborative filtering recommends what similar people liked. Modern systems learn embeddings for users and items. The score is a dot product. New users and new items have no history: the cold start problem. Large systems work in stages: candidates, ranking, and rules. Test with newer history, then with an A/B test. Measure long-term satisfaction, not only clicks. Watch for feedback loops, filter bubbles, wrong goals, bias, and privacy problems."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"A forecast predicts future values from past values. This topic gives a description of the parts of a time series, baselines, features and models, the correct test of a forecast, and ranges of uncertainty."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"the-parts-of-a-time-series","n":"2","t":"The parts of a time series","x":"A time series is a sequence of measurements in time order, for example the sales of each month. Most time series have three parts. Refer to Figure 1. Trend : the slow, long-term increase or decrease. The farm sells a little more each year. Seasonality : a pattern that repeats at the same time each day, week, or year. Mango sales have a peak each April to June. Noise: the random changes that nobody can predict. Figure 1. Monthly sales of mango boxes. Each year has a peak from April to June (the season), and the sales slowly increase (the trend). The forecast continues the trend and the season. The orange range becomes wider further into the future. All values are only examples. The number of steps into the future is the forecast horizon . A forecast for tomorrow is usually much more accurate than a forecast for next year."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"start-with-a-baseline","n":"3","t":"Start with a baseline","x":"Simple forecasts are frequently difficult to beat. Always calculate them first. Refer to Module 7, Section 4 . Baseline Forecast Good when Last value The same as the last measurement. The values change slowly, for example the temperature in the next hour. Same time last season The value of the same month last year, or the same day last week. There is a strong season, for example mango sales. Moving average The mean of the last few values, for example the last 4 weeks. There is much noise and no strong season."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"features-and-models","n":"4","t":"Features and models","x":"Features To use usual ML models, change the time series into a table. Each row is one time step. Useful features are: Lag features: the values of the last steps, for example the sales of last week and of the same week last year. Calendar: the day of the week, the month, holidays, and festivals such as Diwali. Other known values: the price, a planned discount, the weather forecast. Caution Use only values that are known at the time of the forecast. The real weather of next week is not known today. Only the weather forecast is known. Otherwise this is data leakage. Refer to Module 7, Section 3 . Models Model How it works Exponential smoothing A weighted mean of the past. New values have more weight. It can also model trend and season. ARIMA A classic statistical model that uses the past values and the past errors. Gradient boosting Many decision trees, trained on the table with lag and calendar features. Often very good for sales. Neural networks Good when there are thousands of related series, for example the sales of each product in each shop. New pretrained models for time series also exist."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"test-a-forecast-correctly","n":"5","t":"Test a forecast correctly","x":"Do not divide a time series at random. A random division puts future values into the training set. Always train on the past and test on the future. Procedure: backtesting Select a date in the past, for example 1 January 2025. Train the model only with the data before that date. Make a forecast for the next steps, for example 3 months. Compare it with what really occurred. Move the date forward one month, and do steps 2 to 4 again. Calculate the mean error of all the tests. We call this backtesting . It is the cross-validation of time series. Refer to Module 7, Section 5 . Measure the error Month Real Forecast Error Error as % of real April 120 100 20 16.7% May 150 140 10 6.7% June 90 110 20 22.2% MAE (mean absolute error) = (20 + 10 + 20) ÷ 3 = 16.7 boxes. It is in the unit of the data. MAPE (mean absolute percentage error) = (16.7% + 6.7% + 22.2%) ÷ 3 = 15.2%. It lets you compare series of different sizes. Do not use it when real values can be 0."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"give-a-range-not-one-number","n":"6","t":"Give a range, not one number","x":"No forecast is exact. A prediction interval gives a range with a probability. Example: \"Next May: 140 boxes. With 80% probability, between 115 and 165 boxes.\" The range helps people to make decisions: Mangoes spoil quickly. If too many are left, the farm loses money. If there are too few, it loses customers. The range shows the risk on each side. The range becomes wider further into the future. Plan again when new data comes. Note Check the range with backtesting too. If you say \"80%\", then approximately 80% of the real values must be in the ranges."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"where-people-use-forecasting","n":"7","t":"Where people use forecasting","x":"Area Forecast Usual horizon Shops and farms Demand for each product, to order the correct quantity. Days to months Electricity Power use, and power from sun and wind. Hours to days Weather Temperature, rain, storms. New AI weather models now compete with physics models. Hours to 2 weeks Hospitals and call centers The number of patients or calls, to plan the staff. Hours to weeks Transport Traffic and travel time, the number of passengers. Minutes to months Finance Cash flow of a company. Prices of shares are very difficult to forecast. Weeks to years"},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"limits-of-forecasting","n":"8","t":"Limits of forecasting","x":"Sudden events. A pandemic, a strike, a flood, or a new law changes the pattern. A model that learned from the past cannot know this. A person must adjust the forecast. Too little history. To learn a yearly season, you need at least two or three years of data. The forecast changes the future. If the shop orders less because of a low forecast, it also sells less. The data then seems to agree with the forecast. Records of sales are not demand. When a product is sold out, the sales show 0, but people still wanted it."},{"p":"topic-forecasting.html","w":"Topic: Forecasting","id":"summary","n":"9","t":"Summary","x":"A time series has a trend, a season, and noise. Compare each model with simple baselines: the last value, the same time last season, a moving average. Lag and calendar features change a series into a table for usual ML models. Never divide a time series at random. Use backtesting: train on the past, test on the future. MAE is in the unit of the data. MAPE is a percentage. Give a prediction interval, and adjust for sudden events."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"about-the-projects","n":"1","t":"About the projects","x":"Reading teaches you the ideas. Projects teach you to use them. This page has nine projects. Each project uses the ideas of one or more modules. Level What you need Projects Beginner A web browser. No code. 1, 2, and 6 Intermediate A Google account for Google Colab. You copy and run the code. 3 and 4 Advanced Google Colab. You read and change the code. 5, 7, and 8 Advanced, with Git A GitHub account, and Python on your computer or GitHub Codespaces. 9 How to run the code Open Google Colab and sign in. Colab runs Python in your browser. It is free, and you do not need to install software. Select New notebook . On the project, select Copy the code . Paste the code into the first cell of the notebook. Select the run button of the cell, or push Shift and Enter together. Compare your results with \"Check your work\". Small differences are usual. Caution Do not put private data into the projects, for example real customer data, passwords, or messages from other persons. Use the example data, or data that you have permission to use. Each project has a Mark as done button at its end. Your progress is kept in this browser."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-1-teach-a-computer-to-see","n":"2","t":"Project 1: Teach a computer to see","x":"Beginner About 30 min Teachable Machine, a webcam Uses: Module 1, Section 4 Module 3, Section 4 Module 4, Section 9 What you make: an image classifier that tells the difference between two objects, for example a pen and a spoon. You train it with your webcam, in your browser. Steps Open Teachable Machine . Select Get started , then Image project , then Standard image model . Change the names of the two classes, for example \"Pen\" and \"Spoon\". These are the labels. For each class, select Webcam . Hold the object in front of the camera and record approximately 100 images. Turn the object and move it, so that the images are different. Select Train model . The tool uses transfer learning: it starts from a network that is already trained on millions of photographs. In Preview , show each object to the camera. Look at the probabilities. Now test it with things that the model did not see: a different pen, your hand, an empty desk. Check your work The model gives more than 90% to the correct class when you show each object. With an object that is in no class, the model still selects a class. It does not know “nothing”. You can explain why: the model must put each image into one of the classes that you made. Go further Make a test for bias: train with images in bright light only. Then test in dim light. What occurs? Refer to Module 4, Section 9. Add a third class “Nothing” with images of the empty desk. Does this solve the problem above? Record only 10 images for each class. Is the model still good? This shows why the quantity of data is important."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-2-predict-flat-prices-with-a-line","n":"3","t":"Project 2: Predict flat prices with a line","x":"Beginner About 30 min A spreadsheet, for example Google Sheets or Excel Uses: Module 2, Section 4 Module 2, Section 6 Module 3, Section 5 What you make: a linear regression model in a spreadsheet. It predicts the price of a flat from its area. Steps Open a new spreadsheet. In column A, write \"Area (m²)\". In column B, write \"Price (₹ lakh)\". Copy the data below into rows 2 to 11. In cell D2, write =SLOPE(B2:B11, A2:A11) . This is the price for each additional square metre. In cell D3, write =INTERCEPT(B2:B11, A2:A11) . This is where the line crosses zero area. In cell D4, write =RSQ(B2:B11, A2:A11) . This shows how well the line fits the data: 1 is a perfect fit. Predict the price of a flat of 80 m²: write =D3 + D2 * 80 . Make a scatter chart of the data, and add a trend line. The trend line is your model. Area (m²) Price (₹ lakh) 45 58 52 66 60 74 65 82 70 86 78 97 85 104 92 113 100 121 110 135 Check your work The slope is approximately 1.18 and the intercept is approximately 4.6. Thus each square metre adds approximately ₹1.18 lakh. R² is approximately 0.999. The line fits this example data very well. Real data is never this clean. The prediction for 80 m² is approximately ₹98.7 lakh. Go further Predict the price for 150 m². The data has no flat larger than 110 m², so this prediction is not reliable. A model is only good inside the range of its training data. Add a column “Distance to station”. Use =LINEST() to use two features at the same time. Change one price to an error, for example 950. Look at how the slope changes. Refer to outliers in Module 2, Section 4."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-3-build-a-spam-filter","n":"4","t":"Project 3: Build a spam filter","x":"Intermediate About 45 min Python in Google Colab Uses: Module 2, Section 7 Module 3, Section 10 Module 3, Section 4 What you make: a model that reads an SMS message and predicts \"spam\" or \"ham\" (not spam). It learns from 5,572 real messages. The model uses Bayes' rule for each word. Python Copy the code import pandas as pd from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer from sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, precision_score, recall_score, confusion_matrix # 1. Load 5,572 SMS messages. Each message has the label \"spam\" or \"ham\" (not spam). url = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/justmarkham/pycon-2016-tutorial/master/data/sms.tsv\" data = pd.read_csv(url, sep=\"\\t\", header=None, names=[\"label\", \"text\"]) print(data[\"label\"].value_counts()) # 2. Divide the data: 80% for training, 20% for the test. text_train, text_test, label_train, label_test = train_test_split( data[\"text\"], data[\"label\"], test_size=0.2, random_state=1, stratify=data[\"label\"]) # 3. Change each message into word counts (the features). vectorizer = CountVectorizer() X_train_counts = vectorizer.fit_transform(text_train) X_test_counts = vectorizer.transform(text_test) # 4. Train a Naive Bayes model. It uses Bayes' rule for each word. spam_model = MultinomialNB() spam_model.fit(X_train_counts, label_train) # 5. Measure the model on the test set. spam_pred = spam_model.predict(X_test_counts) print(\"Accuracy :\", round(accuracy_score(label_test, spam_pred), 3)) print(\"Precision:\", round(precision_score(label_test, spam_pred, pos_label=\"spam\"), 3)) print(\"Recall :\", round(recall_score(label_test, spam_pred, pos_label=\"spam\"), 3)) print(confusion_matrix(label_test, spam_pred, labels=[\"spam\", \"ham\"])) # 6. Try your own messages. new = [\"You won a free prize! Send your bank details now\", \"Are we still meeting for lunch today?\"] print(spam_model.predict(vectorizer.transform(new))) Check your work The data has 4,825 ham messages and 747 spam messages. The data is not balanced. The accuracy is approximately 0.99, the precision approximately 0.99, and the recall approximately 0.94. In the confusion matrix, approximately 9 spam messages are missed, and approximately 1 good message is marked as spam. Your two messages are predicted as “spam” and “ham”. Go further Write a message that is spam but that the model does not find. Which words help you to trick it? This is how spam senders work. Change CountVectorizer() to CountVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\") . Do the results change? Why is the recall lower than the precision? Which error is worse for a user: a missed spam, or a lost good message?"},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-4-find-groups-of-customers","n":"5","t":"Project 4: Find groups of customers","x":"Intermediate About 40 min Python in Google Colab Uses: Module 3, Section 6 Module 2, Section 5 What you make: a k-means model that finds groups of customers in data that has no labels. Then you find the best number of groups. Python Copy the code import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from sklearn.cluster import KMeans from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler # 1. Make example data: 180 customers with two features each. # Feature 1: visits to the shop in a month. Feature 2: money spent in a month (rupees). rng = np.random.default_rng(0) students = np.column_stack([rng.normal(12, 2, 60), rng.normal(1500, 300, 60)]) families = np.column_stack([rng.normal(6, 1.5, 60), rng.normal(6000, 800, 60)]) workers = np.column_stack([rng.normal(3, 1, 60), rng.normal(3000, 500, 60)]) X = np.vstack([students, families, workers]) # 2. Standardize the features (Module 2, Section 5), so that money does not hide the visits. X_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X) # 3. Try 1 to 6 groups. \"Inertia\" is the total distance from each point to its center. for k in range(1, 7): km = KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(X_scaled) print(k, \"groups: inertia =\", round(km.inertia_, 1)) # 4. Use 3 groups and show them on a chart. km = KMeans(n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(X_scaled) plt.scatter(X[:, 0], X[:, 1], c=km.labels_) plt.xlabel(\"Visits in a month\") plt.ylabel(\"Money spent in a month (rupees)\") plt.show() Check your work The inertia decreases very much from 1 to 3 groups, and only a little after 3. This “elbow” shows that 3 groups is a good number. The chart shows three clear groups: many visits with little money, few visits with much money, and few visits with medium money. You can give each group a name, for example “students”, “families”, and “office workers”. The model does not know these names. A person gives them. Go further Remove the standardization (use X in place of X_scaled ). Are the groups worse? Why? The money values are much larger than the visit values. Change the number of customers in each group to 10. Is the elbow still clear? Use your own data, for example the time and the cost of your trips. Do not use data about other persons without their permission."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-5-a-neural-network-that-reads-digits","n":"6","t":"Project 5: A neural network that reads digits","x":"Advanced About 45 min Python in Google Colab Uses: Module 4, Section 3 Module 4, Section 4 Module 3, Section 9 What you make: a neural network that reads handwritten digits from 0 to 9. The data is part of scikit-learn, so you do not need to download it. Python Copy the code from sklearn.datasets import load_digits from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split from sklearn.neural_network import MLPClassifier from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, confusion_matrix # 1. Load 1,797 small images of handwritten digits. Each image has 8 x 8 = 64 pixels. digits = load_digits() X = digits.data / 16.0 # change the pixel values to numbers from 0 to 1 y = digits.target # the label: the digit 0 to 9 # 2. Divide the data. X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split( X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=1, stratify=y) # 3. Make a neural network: 64 inputs, two hidden layers (64 and 32 neurons), 10 outputs. model = MLPClassifier(hidden_layer_sizes=(64, 32), max_iter=500, random_state=1) model.fit(X_train, y_train) # backpropagation and gradient descent happen here # 4. Measure the model. pred = model.predict(X_test) print(\"Training accuracy:\", round(model.score(X_train, y_train), 3)) print(\"Test accuracy :\", round(accuracy_score(y_test, pred), 3)) print(confusion_matrix(y_test, pred)) Check your work The training accuracy is 1.0 and the test accuracy is approximately 0.98. The difference between the two shows a small quantity of overfitting. Refer to Module 3, Section 9. In the confusion matrix, most errors are between digits that look similar, for example 8 and 1. Go further Change hidden_layer_sizes to (8,) : one small hidden layer. Is the model worse? Then try (256, 128) . Use only 10% of the data for training ( test_size=0.9 ). How much does the test accuracy decrease? Show the images that the model gets wrong. Would a person also make these errors?"},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-6-a-prompt-lab","n":"7","t":"Project 6: A prompt lab","x":"Beginner About 45 min Any chat AI and a spreadsheet Uses: Module 5, Section 7 Module 5, Section 5 Module 6, Section 6 What you make: a prompt for a real task, and a test that measures how good the prompt is. This is how professionals improve prompts: they measure, they do not guess. Steps Select a task with a clear correct answer. Example: put customer messages into one of three categories: \"Question\", \"Complaint\", or \"Order\". In a spreadsheet, write 10 test messages. For each message, write the correct category. Include 3 difficult messages, for example a complaint that is also a question. Write prompt version 1: only the task. Use the prompt with each test message. Write the answer of the AI in the spreadsheet. Count the correct answers. Write prompt version 2: add the context, the format (\"Answer with one word\"), and two examples (few-shot prompting). Test version 2 with the same 10 messages. Compare the scores. Test version 2 again. Do you get the same answers? If not, use a lower temperature if the tool lets you. Check your work You have a table with 10 messages, the correct answers, and the answers from each version. Version 2 has a higher score than version 1, especially on the difficult messages. You can tell which part of version 2 helped the most. Go further Add a message that is in no category. Tell the model to answer “Other”. Does it obey? Use the same test with a different chat AI. Which one is better for your task? Make the test set larger: 30 messages. Is the difference between the versions still the same?"},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-7-audit-a-model-for-bias","n":"8","t":"Project 7: Audit a model for bias","x":"Advanced About 60 min Python in Google Colab Uses: Module 6, Section 3 Module 3, Section 10 Module 6, Section 6 What you make: a test that finds if your spam filter from Project 3 works equally well for different groups of messages. Then you write a short model card : a description of the model for its users. Python Copy the code # Continue from Project 3 (the spam filter). Run this code after the code of Project 3. from sklearn.metrics import recall_score, precision_score test = pd.DataFrame({\"text\": text_test, \"label\": label_test, \"pred\": spam_pred}) test[\"group\"] = test[\"text\"].str.len().apply(lambda n: \"short (< 60 characters)\" if n < 60 else \"long\") for group, part in test.groupby(\"group\"): print(group, \"| messages:\", len(part), \"| spam:\", (part[\"label\"] == \"spam\").sum(), \"| recall:\", round(recall_score(part[\"label\"], part[\"pred\"], pos_label=\"spam\", zero_division=0), 3), \"| precision:\", round(precision_score(part[\"label\"], part[\"pred\"], pos_label=\"spam\", zero_division=0), 3)) Check your work The recall for long messages is approximately 0.95. The recall for short messages is approximately 0.75. The test set has only approximately 8 short spam messages. A result from 8 examples is not reliable. Collect more examples before you decide. You can explain the risk: if spam senders use short messages, many of them will not be found. Write the model card Write one short paragraph for each heading: Use: what the model is for, and what it is not for. Data: where the training data comes from, and how old it is. Results: accuracy, precision, and recall, for all messages and for each group. Limits: where the model is weak, for example short messages or other languages. Responsibility: who checks the model, and who users can contact. Go further Make a different group: messages with numbers and messages without numbers. Is there a difference? Your model was trained on English messages. Test it on messages in a different language. What occurs? Use the capstone project to plan a better spam filter. Refer to Module 6, Section 8."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-8-find-drift-and-train-again","n":"9","t":"Project 8: Find drift and train again","x":"Advanced About 45 min Python in Google Colab MLOps Uses: Module 7, Section 7 Module 8, Section 3 Module 8, Section 4 What you make: a monitoring loop for the mango checker. Each month, it compares the new data with the training data and measures the recall. When it finds a problem, it trains a new model (the challenger ), compares it with the model in production (the champion ), and updates a small model registry. In month 4, a new camera comes to the farm. It sees fewer spots on the mangoes. Python Copy the code import numpy as np from scipy.stats import ks_2samp from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier from sklearn.metrics import recall_score rng = np.random.default_rng(7) def make_month(n, new_camera=False): \"\"\"Example data for the mangoes of one month: features X and labels y (1 = damaged).\"\"\" weight = rng.normal(250, 40, n) firmness = rng.normal(6, 1.5, n) spots = rng.poisson(1.5, n) y = ((spots >= 4) | (firmness < 3.5)).astype(int) if new_camera: spots = np.maximum(spots - 2, 0) # the new camera sees fewer spots return np.column_stack([weight, firmness, spots]), y # 1. Train the first model on 3,000 old mangoes. Put it in the registry. X_train, y_train = make_month(3000) model = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=1).fit(X_train, y_train) registry = [{\"version\": 1, \"month\": 0, \"status\": \"production\"}] # 2. Each month: compare the new data with the training data, and measure the recall. for month in range(1, 7): X, y = make_month(1000, new_camera=(month >= 4)) p = ks_2samp(X_train[:, 2], X[:, 2]).pvalue # drift test for the \"spots\" feature recall = recall_score(y, model.predict(X)) alert = p < 0.01 or recall < 0.90 print(f\"Month {month}: drift p-value = {p:.3f}, recall = {recall:.2f}\", \"<- ALERT\" if alert else \"\") if not alert: continue # 3. Alert: train a challenger with half of the new data only. The old data comes # from the old camera, so it does not agree with the new data any more. # Compare the two models on the other half, which neither model saw. half = len(y) // 2 X_new, y_new = X[:half], y[:half] challenger = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=1).fit(X_new, y_new) old_r = recall_score(y[half:], model.predict(X[half:])) new_r = recall_score(y[half:], challenger.predict(X[half:])) print(f\" Champion recall {old_r:.2f}, challenger recall {new_r:.2f}\") # 4. Promote the challenger only if it is clearly better. if new_r >= old_r + 0.02: registry[-1][\"status\"] = \"archived\" registry.append({\"version\": len(registry) + 1, \"month\": month, \"status\": \"production\"}) model, X_train, y_train = challenger, X_new, y_new print(f\" Version {len(registry)} is now in production.\") print(registry) The drift test (the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test) compares two sets of values. A small p-value, for example less than 0.01, means that the two sets very probably do not come from the same distribution. Refer to Module 2, Section 5 . Check your work Months 1 to 3 have a large p-value and a recall of approximately 1.00. There is no alert. In month 4, the p-value is 0.000 and the recall decreases to approximately 0.47. The alert starts. The champion has a recall of approximately 0.47 on the test half. The challenger has approximately 1.00. Version 2 goes into production, and version 1 is archived. Months 5 and 6 have no alert, because the drift test now compares with the new training data. You can explain why the challenger uses only the new data: the old data comes from a different camera. Go further Remove the drift test, and keep only the recall check. In real use, labels come late. Which check finds the problem first? Change the camera effect from spots - 2 to spots - 1 . Does the drift test still find it? Does the recall decrease as much? Add the data version and the recall to each entry of the registry, and save the registry as a JSON file. Try a professional tool for the same work, for example MLflow for experiments and a model registry."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"project-9-ship-a-model-with-ci-cd","n":"10","t":"Project 9: Ship a model with CI/CD","x":"Advanced About 90 min A GitHub account, and Python on your computer or GitHub Codespaces MLOps Uses: Module 8, Section 5 Module 8, Section 6 Module 8, Section 7 What you make: a repository on GitHub with the mango checker service from Module 8. Each change goes through an automatic pipeline that trains the model, runs the tests, and builds the container image. Then you send a bad model, and you see the pipeline stop it. This project is a good first item for your portfolio. Refer to Careers, Section 5 . Steps On GitHub, make a new public repository with the name mango-checker . Select Add a README file . Open the repository in a codespace: select Code , then Codespaces , then Create codespace . A codespace is a computer in your browser with Python and Git. You can also copy the repository to your own computer with git clone . Make the six files of Module 8 in the repository: train.py and app.py ( Section 5 ), requirements.txt and Dockerfile ( Section 6 ), test_app.py and .github/workflows/ci.yml ( Section 7 ). Use the Copy buttons. Make a file .gitignore with the two lines below. The pipeline makes the model again each time, so the model file does not go into Git. .gitignore Copy model.joblib model_info.json In the terminal, test everything on your computer first: Terminal Copy the commands pip install -r requirements.txt python train.py pytest Save the files in Git, and send them to GitHub: Terminal Copy the commands git add . git commit -m \"Add the mango checker service and its pipeline\" git push On GitHub, open the Actions tab. Look at the pipeline run. Select it to see each step: install, train, test, and build. Now send a bad model. Make a branch with git switch -c weak-model . In train.py , change RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=1) to RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=1, random_state=1) . Commit the change, push the branch, and open a pull request. Look at the pipeline of the pull request. Read the error of the failed test. Then close the pull request without merging it. Check your work On your computer, python train.py prints a recall of 0.98, and pytest shows 5 passed tests. On GitHub, the pipeline of the main branch has a green check mark. The pipeline of the pull request has a red mark. The weak model has a recall of 0.041, and the error is assert 0.041 >= 0.9 . You can explain an important point: the four API tests still passed with the weak model. Only the quality gate found the problem. Tests of the code alone are not sufficient for an AI service. Go further Add a load test: a test that sends 100 requests and fails if the slowest answer takes more than 100 ms. Refer to the story in Module 8, Part 8 . Make the pipeline send the container image to GitHub Container Registry, with the version as its tag. Put the service on a cloud host that has a free plan, and add the address to your README. Write the README with the template in Careers, Section 5 ."},{"p":"projects.html","w":"Projects","id":"after-the-projects","n":"11","t":"After the projects","x":"Keep your work. A small set of projects shows employers and teachers what you can do. Save each notebook. In Colab, select File , then Save a copy in GitHub or Download . For each project, write three sentences: the problem, your result, and what you learned. Include what did not work. Honest results are more useful than perfect results. Then plan your own project with the capstone. Refer to Module 6, Section 8 ."},{"p":"review.html","w":"Final review","id":"how-the-final-review-works","n":"1","t":"How the final review works","x":"Each try contains approximately 3 questions from each module. The questions and the order of the answers change each time. You must get 75% or more (15 of 20) to pass. This is more than the 70% of a module knowledge check. After each answer, an explanation tells you which module and section to read again. You can try again as many times as you want. Your best score is kept. Note Do the knowledge check of each module first. To get the certificate, you must pass all eight knowledge checks and the final review. Your knowledge checks: Before you start, read the cheat sheets . They contain the most important points of each module on one page."},{"p":"review.html","w":"Final review","id":"after-the-review","n":"3","t":"After the review","x":"Get your certificate . Do the projects . They change what you know into what you can do. Read a short topic, for example computer vision or forecasting . Find a job direction and the next things to learn in Careers and next steps ."},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-1-artificial-intelligence","n":"1","t":"Module 1: Artificial intelligence","x":"Key ideas AI is software that does tasks that usually need a human mind. The parts: AI > machine learning > deep learning > generative AI. Each is a part of the larger area. In ML, the computer finds the rules in data. A person does not write the rules. Training is a loop: guess, compare, adjust, repeat. A chat AI predicts the next token again and again. Remember All AI today is narrow AI. AGI does not exist at this time. A neural network has layers of neurons. Weights connect the neurons. AI became strong when data, fast chips, and better methods came together. Dates: 1950 Turing test, 1956 the name \"AI\", 2012 deep learning, 2017 transformer, 2022 public chat AI. Watch out AI can make errors and can sound sure when it is wrong (hallucination). It can learn bias from its data. Always do a check of important information. Print this sheet Open Module 1"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-2-mathematics","n":"2","t":"Module 2: Mathematics for machine learning","x":"Statistics and probability A model is a function with parameters. Training finds good values for them. Mean, median, and standard deviation describe data. The median is not changed much by outliers. Many measurements have a normal distribution: 68% of values are less than 1 standard deviation from the mean, 95% less than 2. Correlation shows that two values change together. It does not show cause. Bayes' rule changes a probability with new information. Rare events give many false alarms. Vectors and calculus A vector is a list of numbers. A matrix is a table of numbers. A neural layer multiplies a matrix by a vector, and adds a bias. Softmax changes scores into probabilities. Cross-entropy measures the loss. The derivative is the slope. Gradient descent moves opposite to the slope. The chain rule makes backpropagation possible. Watch out Standardize features that have very different sizes. Otherwise the largest feature hides the others. For all formulas, refer to Sheet 9, Formula sheet . Print this sheet Open Module 2"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-3-machine-learning","n":"3","t":"Module 3: Machine learning","x":"The workflow Define the problem. Collect data. Prepare the data, and divide it: training, validation, test. Select a type of model. Train the model. Evaluate it with data that it did not see. Put it into use. Monitor its results. Types of learning Supervised: examples with labels. Classification (a category) or regression (a number). Unsupervised: no labels. Find groups (clustering). Reinforcement: actions and rewards. Fit Underfitting: errors on training and new data. Model too simple. Overfitting: good on training, bad on new data. Use more data, a simpler model, or stop early. Measure a classifier Accuracy: correct predictions ÷ all predictions. Precision: TP ÷ (TP + FP). Important when a false alarm is expensive. Recall: TP ÷ (TP + FN). Important when a missed item is dangerous. High accuracy is not always good when one category is rare. Common methods Linear regression: a straight line. Prices. Logistic regression: a probability for a category. Decision tree and random forest: yes/no questions, many trees vote. k-nearest neighbors: the most similar examples. k-means: k groups around k centers. Print this sheet Open Module 3"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-4-deep-learning","n":"4","t":"Module 4: Deep learning","x":"Key ideas Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers. The network finds the features in the raw data. A neuron multiplies its inputs by weights, adds a bias, and applies an activation function. Backpropagation finds the gradient for each weight. Gradient descent adjusts the weights. Embeddings change items into lists of numbers. Similar items have similar embeddings. Transformers use attention to connect each token to the other tokens. Types of network Feedforward: numbers in a table. CNN: images. Filters find patterns. RNN: sequences, one item at a time. Mostly replaced by transformers. Transformer: text, and now also images and sound. In practice Training needs GPUs. To prevent overfitting, use dropout, data augmentation, and early stopping. With little data, use transfer learning: start from a network that is already trained, and train it a little more on your data. Print this sheet Open Module 4"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-5-generative-ai","n":"5","t":"Module 5: Generative AI","x":"How it is made and how it answers Generative AI makes new text, images, sound, video, and code. Three stages: pretraining, fine-tuning, human feedback. The context window limits how much text the model can use at one time. An image generator starts from noise and removes it step by step. RAG lets a model use your documents and show its sources. Temperature Low (0 to 0.3): almost the same answer each time. Facts, code, data. Medium (0.5 to 0.8): usual chat and writing. High (1.0 or more): varied, more errors. Ideas and stories. A good prompt The task (always necessary). The context and the audience. The format of the answer. One to three examples (few-shot prompting). For logic, ask for the steps. Divide a large task. Watch out AI-made content can be false. Always do a check before you use it. Do not put private data into a public AI tool. Text in web pages and documents can contain hidden instructions (prompt injection). Print this sheet Open Module 5"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-6-responsible-ai","n":"6","t":"Module 6: Responsible AI","x":"Principles Fairness: works equally well for all groups. Transparency: people know that AI is used. Privacy: personal data only with permission. Safety: no physical or financial harm. Accountability: a person is responsible, not the AI. Human oversight: a person can check, change, or stop decisions. Bias and law Bias comes from the data, the labels, the design, or the use. Measure the results for each group separately. India: DPDP Act (2023). EU: GDPR (2018) and the EU AI Act (2024), with four levels of risk. Data minimization: collect only the data that you need. Do not use AI when A simple rule works. There is no good data. Nobody can check the errors. The law requires an explanation that you cannot give. The task needs human care. The cost is larger than the benefit. \"Do not use AI\" is a correct result of a careful plan. Print this sheet Open Module 6"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-7-ai-in-practice","n":"7","t":"Module 7: AI in practice","x":"Before training Missing values: remove, fill with the median (imputation), or add a \"missing\" feature. Categories: one-hot encoding, not 1, 2, 3. Data leakage: divide the data first. Use only information that is available at the time of the prediction. Compare each model with a simple baseline. Use cross-validation to select hyperparameters. Use the test set only one time. After training Ways of deployment: batch, online, on the device. Release: version number, shadow mode, A/B test, then all users. Keep the old version. Data drift: the inputs change. Concept drift: the correct answers change. Monitor real results, set alert limits, and train again with new data. LLM applications and agents Test each new prompt or model with a fixed evaluation set. Use guardrails before and after the model. Calculate the cost: tokens × price. Use an agent only when the steps must change. Give it limited tools, a maximum number of steps, a record of each step, and human approval for important actions. Print this sheet Open Module 7"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"sheet-8-devops-for-ai","n":"8","t":"Module 8: DevOps for AI","x":"Key ideas DevOps: one team builds and operates, with small changes, automation, and fast feedback. MLOps adds versions of data and models, model tests, and training again. LLMOps adds prompt versions, evaluation sets in the pipeline, and cost monitoring. Keep a version of code, data, models, prompts, and libraries. Build and release Serve the model as an API. Check each input. Send the model version with each answer. Pin library versions. Put the service in a container. Environments: development, staging, production. Pipeline: tests, build, staging, canary, production. Quality gates stop bad models. Operate Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards, alerts. SLO example: 99.9% availability = 43.2 minutes without service in 30 days. Use the p95 latency, not the mean. Incident: confirm, roll back, find the cause, repair, write a postmortem. Watch out Never put secrets in code or Git. If one gets in, change it immediately. Give each service the least access. Set a cost budget and an alert in the cloud. Keep personal data out of the logs. Print this sheet Open Module 8"},{"p":"cheat-sheets.html","w":"Cheat sheets","id":"formula-sheet","n":"9","t":"Formula sheet","x":"All formulas of Module 2 and the measurements of Module 3 . Σ means \"add all the values\". Statistics Mean mean = Σx ÷ n Median the middle value after you sort the values With an even number of values: the mean of the two middle values. Standard deviation √( Σ(x − mean)² ÷ n ) z-score z = (x − mean) ÷ standard deviation Used for standardization. Correlation r from −1 to +1 +1: up together. 0: no straight-line relation. −1: one up, one down. Probability Probability P = favorable results ÷ all results Always from 0 to 1. Not A P(not A) = 1 − P(A) Bayes' rule P(A | B) = P(B | A) × P(A) ÷ P(B) Softmax p i = e s i ÷ Σ e s j Changes scores into probabilities that add up to 1. Cross-entropy loss = −log(p of the correct answer) Small when the model gives the correct answer a high probability. Vectors and matrices Dot product a · b = a 1 b 1 + a 2 b 2 + … Length |a| = √(a · a) Cosine similarity (a · b) ÷ (|a| × |b|) 1: same direction. 0: not related. Neural layer outputs = activation( W · x + b ) Calculus and training Slope change in y ÷ change in x Example derivative f(x) = x² → f′(x) = 2x Gradient descent new weight = weight − learning rate × slope Chain rule slope of y for x = (slope of y for u) × (slope of u for x) Measure a model Accuracy (TP + TN) ÷ all predictions Precision TP ÷ (TP + FP) Recall TP ÷ (TP + FN) Mean absolute error Σ|real − predicted| ÷ n For regression and forecasts. Refer to the forecasting topic . TP: true positive. TN: true negative. FP: false positive (false alarm). FN: false negative (missed item). Print this sheet Open Module 2"},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"scope","n":"1","t":"Scope","x":"This course gives you the base of AI. A job usually needs more: practice with real tools, a portfolio of projects, and knowledge of one area of work. This page helps you to select a direction and to plan the next steps. Note Not all AI jobs need advanced mathematics or programming. Product, design, law, and domain jobs also use AI every day. Your knowledge of an area, for example farming, health, or finance, is valuable together with AI."},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"jobs-in-ai","n":"2","t":"Jobs in AI","x":"Data analyst Finds answers in data with tables, charts, and statistics. Tells managers what the data shows. Main skills SQL Spreadsheets Statistics Charts Start with Module 2 , Project 2 Data scientist Makes models that predict or explain. Selects the method, tests it, and explains the results. Main skills Python Statistics ML methods Communication Start with Module 3 , Project 3 Machine learning engineer Puts models into products. Makes them fast, reliable, and monitored. Main skills Python Software engineering Deep learning Cloud Start with Module 8 , Project 9 AI application engineer Builds products on large language models: prompts, RAG, tools, agents, and evaluation. Main skills Programming LLM APIs Evaluation Security Start with Module 5 , Project 6 Data engineer Builds the pipelines that collect, clean, and store data, so that other teams can use it. Main skills SQL Python Databases Cloud Start with Module 7, Section 3 Research scientist Finds new methods and new types of model. Usually needs a master's degree or a PhD. Main skills Advanced mathematics Deep learning Experiments Writing Start with Module 2 , Module 4 AI product manager Decides what to build and why. Connects users, engineers, and business goals. Main skills User needs Evaluation Communication Risk Start with The capstone , Module 7 AI governance and ethics specialist Makes sure that AI systems are fair, legal, and safe. Writes policies and does audits. Main skills Law and policy Bias testing Risk Writing Start with Module 6 , Project 7 AI in your own job Teachers, doctors, designers, lawyers, and farmers use AI tools to work better. They know where AI helps and where it fails. Main skills Your area Prompts Checks Judgment Start with Module 5, Section 7"},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"skills-for-each-job","n":"3","t":"Skills for each job","x":"This table shows how much of each skill five usual jobs need. \"High\" means that you use the skill every day. Skill Data analyst Data scientist ML engineer AI application engineer AI product manager Python programming Medium High High High Basic SQL and data High High Medium Medium Basic Statistics High High Medium Basic Medium ML methods Basic High High Medium Medium Deep learning Basic Medium High Medium Basic LLMs, prompts, RAG Medium Medium Medium High Medium Software engineering and cloud Basic Medium High High Basic Evaluation and monitoring Medium High High High High Responsible AI and law Medium Medium Medium Medium High Communication High High Medium Medium High Figure 1. Skills for five usual AI jobs. This is a general guide. Each company is different."},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"what-to-learn-next","n":"4","t":"What to learn next","x":"Do the steps in this order. Each step takes approximately one to three months with a few hours each week. Make one small project in each step. Programming and data. Learn Python, the pandas library for tables, and SQL for databases. Learn to use Git to keep versions of your code. Machine learning in practice. Use scikit-learn with real data sets. Practice data preparation, baselines, and cross-validation. Refer to Module 7 . Deep learning. Learn PyTorch. Train a small image or text model, then use transfer learning. Applications with LLMs. Use an LLM through its API. Build a RAG system, write an evaluation set, and add guardrails. Deployment. Put a model behind an API, in a container, with a CI/CD pipeline, and monitor it. Refer to Module 8 . Free places to learn Resource Good for The Python tutorial Step 1: the official introduction to Python. Kaggle Learn Steps 1 and 2: short courses in the browser, and real data sets. Google Machine Learning Crash Course Step 2: ML with exercises. scikit-learn user guide Step 2: all the usual ML methods, with examples. fast.ai: Practical Deep Learning Step 3: deep learning that starts with working code. PyTorch tutorials Step 3: the main deep learning library. Hugging Face courses Steps 3 and 4: transformers, language models, and agents. Also read the short topics of this manual. They show how AI is used in four large areas of work."},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"make-a-portfolio-from-your-projects","n":"5","t":"Make a portfolio from your projects","x":"A portfolio shows what you can do. For many employers, it is more important than a certificate. Use the nine projects of this manual as a start, and then add a project of your own. Procedure: present a project Make a free account on GitHub. Make one repository for each project. Put the notebook or the code into the repository. Make sure that it runs from the start to the end. Write a README file with the template below. Change the project a little, so that it is your own work. For example, use a different data set, or compare two models. Add a link to the repository in your CV and in your professional profiles. README template (Markdown) Copy the template # Spam filter for SMS messages ## The problem Find spam messages automatically, so that people do not see them. ## The data 5,572 SMS messages with the labels \"spam\" and \"ham\" (SMS Spam Collection, UCI). 13.4% of the messages are spam. ## What I did 1. Divided the data: 80% training, 20% test, with the same share of spam in each. 2. Baseline: always predict \"ham\". Accuracy 86.6%, recall 0%. 3. Model: word counts and Naive Bayes. ## Results | Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | |-----------|----------|-----------|--------| | Baseline | 0.866 | - | 0.00 | | My model | 0.991 | 0.993 | 0.94 | ## Limits Recall is lower for short messages (0.75) than for long messages (0.95). The data is more than ten years old. New types of spam can be different. ## What I learned Accuracy alone is not a good measurement when one category is rare. Note Employers like honest limits. A README that tells what did not work, and why, shows that you understand the model."},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"prepare-for-interviews","n":"6","t":"Prepare for interviews","x":"These questions are frequent in interviews for AI jobs. For each question, practice a short answer in simple words. Then give an example from your projects. Question Read What is the difference between precision and recall? When is each more important? Module 3, Section 10 What is overfitting, and how do you prevent it? Module 3, Section 9 What is data leakage? Give an example. Module 7, Section 3 Why do you need a validation set and a test set? Module 7, Section 5 How does gradient descent work? Module 2, Section 11 How does attention in a transformer work? Module 4, Section 8 How do you test an application that uses an LLM? Module 7, Section 8 How do you find bias in a model? Module 6, Section 3 Tell us about a project. What would you do differently? Your README Use the cheat sheets and the flashcards to revise the main ideas and technical names."},{"p":"careers.html","w":"Careers","id":"summary","n":"7","t":"Summary","x":"There are many AI jobs. Some need much programming, and some need knowledge of users, law, or one area of work. Select a direction, then learn the skills of that job in steps: programming and data, ML, deep learning, LLM applications, deployment. Make a portfolio. Put each project on GitHub with a clear README, a baseline, results, and honest limits. Practice short, simple answers to the usual interview questions, with examples from your projects."},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-a-b-test","t":"A/B test","x":"A test that gives a new version to a small group of users and compares their results with the other users.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-accountability","t":"Accountability","x":"A person or an organization is responsible for the results of an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-accuracy","t":"Accuracy","x":"The percentage of all predictions that are correct.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-activation-function","t":"Activation function","x":"A simple function that a neuron applies to its total.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-agent","t":"Agent","x":"The program that learns and makes decisions in reinforcement learning.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-ai-agent","t":"AI agent","x":"An AI system that uses an LLM to plan steps and use tools to complete a task.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-api","t":"API","x":"Application programming interface: a defined way for one program to ask another program for data or an action.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-attention","t":"Attention","x":"A method that lets each token use information from the other tokens.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-autoscaling","t":"Autoscaling","x":"The automatic change of the number of running copies of a service, to agree with the number of requests.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-backpropagation","t":"Backpropagation","x":"A method that calculates the gradient for each weight, from the output back to the input.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-backtesting","t":"Backtesting","x":"A test of a forecast method on past data: train on the data before a date, forecast after it, and compare. Repeat for many dates.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-base-model","t":"Base model","x":"A model after pretraining only. It continues text but does not follow instructions well.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-baseline","t":"Baseline","x":"A very simple method that gives a minimum result to compare with.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-batch","t":"Batch","x":"A small group of training examples for one update of the weights.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-bayes-rule","t":"Bayes' rule","x":"A rule to calculate P(A | B) from P(B | A), P(A), and P(B).","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-bias","t":"Bias","x":"A number that a neuron adds to its total.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-bounding-box","t":"Bounding box","x":"A rectangle around an object. Four numbers give its position and size.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-branch","t":"Branch","x":"A separate line of work in Git. You join it into the main branch after review.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-canary-release","t":"Canary release","x":"A release of a new version to a small part of the users first.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-candidate-generation","t":"Candidate generation","x":"The first stage of a recommendation system. A fast method selects some hundreds or thousands of possible items.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-chain-rule","t":"Chain rule","x":"A rule to find the slope through many steps: multiply the slopes of the steps.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-ci-cd","t":"CI/CD","x":"Continuous integration and continuous delivery: each change is tested, packaged, and released automatically.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-classification","t":"Classification","x":"Supervised learning that predicts a category.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-clustering","t":"Clustering","x":"A task that puts similar examples into the same group.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cnn","t":"CNN","x":"Convolutional neural network. It uses filters to find patterns in images.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cold-start","t":"Cold start","x":"The problem that a new user or a new item has no history.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-collaborative-filtering","t":"Collaborative filtering","x":"A method that recommends items that similar people liked.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-computer-vision","t":"Computer vision","x":"The part of AI that gets information from images and video.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-concept-drift","t":"Concept drift","x":"A change in the relation between the inputs and the correct answer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-conditional-probability","t":"Conditional probability","x":"The probability of an event when you know that a different event occurred.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-confusion-matrix","t":"Confusion matrix","x":"A table that compares the predictions with the correct answers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-container","t":"Container","x":"A package that contains a program and everything that it needs to run.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-content-based-filtering","t":"Content-based filtering","x":"A method that recommends items that are similar to the items that the person liked before.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-context-window","t":"Context window","x":"The maximum quantity of text, in tokens, that a model can use at one time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-correlation","t":"Correlation","x":"A measurement of how strongly two values change together, between −1 and +1.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cosine-similarity","t":"Cosine similarity","x":"The dot product of two vectors divided by the product of their lengths.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cross-entropy","t":"Cross-entropy","x":"A loss for classification: −log(probability of the correct answer).","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-cross-validation","t":"Cross-validation","x":"A method that trains and tests k times, each time on a different part of the data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data","t":"Data","x":"The examples that a computer uses to learn.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data-augmentation","t":"Data augmentation","x":"A method that makes more training examples from the existing examples.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data-drift","t":"Data drift","x":"A change in the input data after deployment.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data-leakage","t":"Data leakage","x":"Training data that contains information that is not available in real use, or that comes from the test set.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-data-minimization","t":"Data minimization","x":"The principle to collect and keep only the personal data that a system really needs.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-deep-learning","t":"Deep learning","x":"Machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-deepfake","t":"Deepfake","x":"A false image, video, or voice of a real person, made by AI.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-deployment","t":"Deployment","x":"The work that makes a trained model available to its users.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-derivative","t":"Derivative","x":"The slope of a function at one point.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-devops","t":"DevOps","x":"A way of working in which the same team builds, tests, releases, and operates software, with much automation.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-diffusion-model","t":"Diffusion model","x":"A model that makes images when it removes noise step by step.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-distribution","t":"Distribution","x":"A description of how frequently each value occurs.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-dockerfile","t":"Dockerfile","x":"A text file with the steps to build a container image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-dot-product","t":"Dot product","x":"The sum of the products of the numbers in the same positions of two vectors.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-dpdp-act","t":"DPDP Act","x":"India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-dropout","t":"Dropout","x":"A method that switches off random neurons during training.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-embedding","t":"Embedding","x":"A list of numbers that shows the meaning of an item.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-epoch","t":"Epoch","x":"One complete pass through all of the training data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-eu-ai-act","t":"EU AI Act","x":"The European Union law about AI, adopted in 2024. It has four levels of risk.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-evaluation-set","t":"Evaluation set","x":"A fixed set of example inputs with good answers, to test each new version.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-explainability","t":"Explainability","x":"The ability to tell people why an AI system made a decision.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-fairness","t":"Fairness","x":"The system works equally well for all groups of people.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-feature","t":"Feature","x":"An input value that the model uses to make a prediction.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-feature-map","t":"Feature map","x":"The grid of numbers that a filter makes.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-feedback-loop","t":"Feedback loop","x":"A loop in which the outputs of a model change the data that the model learns from next time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-few-shot-prompting","t":"Few-shot prompting","x":"A prompt method that gives the model some examples of the task.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-filter","t":"Filter","x":"A small grid of weights that finds one type of pattern in an image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-fine-tuning","t":"Fine-tuning","x":"Additional training on a smaller and more specific set of data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-forecast","t":"Forecast","x":"A prediction of future values of a time series.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-forecast-horizon","t":"Forecast horizon","x":"How far into the future a forecast predicts.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-forward-pass","t":"Forward pass","x":"The calculation from the input layer to the output layer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-foundation-model","t":"Foundation model","x":"A very large general model that people adapt for many tasks.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-function","t":"Function","x":"A rule that changes each input into exactly one output.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gdpr","t":"GDPR","x":"General Data Protection Regulation: the EU law about personal data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-git","t":"Git","x":"A version control system that keeps each change to the files.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gpu","t":"GPU","x":"Graphics processing unit. A chip that does many calculations at the same time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gradient","t":"Gradient","x":"The slope of the loss.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-gradient-descent","t":"Gradient descent","x":"A method that adjusts the weights step by step to decrease the loss.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-guardrail","t":"Guardrail","x":"A check before or after the model that stops unsafe inputs and outputs.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-hallucination","t":"Hallucination","x":"An answer that sounds correct but is false or invented.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-human-in-the-loop","t":"Human in the loop","x":"A design in which a person approves each decision before it has an effect.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-human-oversight","t":"Human oversight","x":"People can check, change, or stop the decisions of an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-hyperparameter","t":"Hyperparameter","x":"A setting that a person selects before training.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-imputation","t":"Imputation","x":"To fill missing values with an estimate, for example the median.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-independent","t":"Independent","x":"Two events are independent when one does not change the probability of the other.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-infrastructure-as-code","t":"Infrastructure as code","x":"A method in which files describe the servers and networks, so they can be reviewed, versioned, and made again.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-iou","t":"IoU","x":"Intersection over union: the overlap area of two boxes divided by the area of both boxes together.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-key-point","t":"Key point","x":"An important point on an object, for example a knee or an elbow of a person.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-kubernetes","t":"Kubernetes","x":"A system that runs many containers on many servers and manages them automatically.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-label","t":"Label","x":"The correct answer for an example.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-latency","t":"Latency","x":"The time between a request and its answer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-learning-rate","t":"Learning rate","x":"A setting that controls the size of each step in gradient descent.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-llm-large-language-model","t":"LLM (large language model)","x":"A very large neural network that is trained on text.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-llmops","t":"LLMOps","x":"MLOps for applications that use large language models: prompt versions, evaluation sets, guardrails, and cost monitoring.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-loss","t":"Loss","x":"A number that shows how wrong the model is.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-machine-translation","t":"Machine translation","x":"A task that changes text from one language into another language.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-mae","t":"MAE","x":"Mean absolute error: the mean of the sizes of the errors, in the unit of the data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-mape","t":"MAPE","x":"Mean absolute percentage error: the mean of the errors as a percentage of the real values.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-matrix","t":"Matrix","x":"A table of numbers with rows and columns.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-mean","t":"Mean","x":"The sum of all values, divided by the number of values.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-median","t":"Median","x":"The middle value when the values are in order.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-mlops","t":"MLOps","x":"DevOps for machine learning: versions of data and models, automatic model tests, and training again.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-modality","t":"Modality","x":"One type of data, for example text, image, sound, or video.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-mode","t":"Mode","x":"The value that occurs most frequently.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-model","t":"Model","x":"The software that training makes. It contains the learned rules as numbers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-model-registry","t":"Model registry","x":"A store for trained models, with their versions, data, test results, and status.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-multimodal","t":"Multimodal","x":"Able to use more than one modality.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-named-entity-recognition","t":"Named entity recognition","x":"A task that finds names of people, places, organizations, dates, and amounts in text.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-natural-language-processing","t":"Natural language processing","x":"The part of AI that works with human language: text and speech.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-neural-network","t":"Neural network","x":"Software with many connected units in layers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-noise","t":"Noise","x":"Random values that hide the content of an image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-non-maximum-suppression","t":"Non-maximum suppression","x":"A step that keeps the box with the highest confidence and removes the other boxes that overlap it.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-normal-distribution","t":"Normal distribution","x":"A bell-shaped distribution. Most values are near the mean.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-object-detection","t":"Object detection","x":"A task that finds each object in an image and gives a box and a label for each one.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-observability","t":"Observability","x":"The ability to know what occurs inside a running system from its logs, metrics, and traces.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-ocr","t":"OCR","x":"Optical character recognition: a task that changes text in an image into text that a computer can use.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-one-hot-encoding","t":"One-hot encoding","x":"A method that changes one feature with categories into columns with 0 or 1.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-outlier","t":"Outlier","x":"A value that is very different from the other values.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-overfitting","t":"Overfitting","x":"The model remembers the training data and does not work well on new data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-parameter","t":"Parameter","x":"A number inside a function that training can change.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-partial-derivative","t":"Partial derivative","x":"The slope for one input, when the other inputs do not change.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-personal-data","t":"Personal data","x":"Data that can identify a person, for example a name, a face, or a location.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-pipeline","t":"Pipeline","x":"A sequence of automatic steps that each change goes through.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-precision","t":"Precision","x":"Of all positive predictions, the percentage that is correct.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-precision-at-k","t":"Precision at k","x":"Of the first k recommended items, the part that the user really used.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-prediction-interval","t":"Prediction interval","x":"A range in which the real value will probably be, with a stated probability.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-pretraining","t":"Pretraining","x":"The first and largest stage of training. The model learns to predict the next token.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-probability","t":"Probability","x":"A number between 0 and 1 that shows how possible an event is.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-prompt","t":"Prompt","x":"The text that you give to an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-prompt-injection","t":"Prompt injection","x":"An attack in which hidden text gives instructions to an AI system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-pull-request","t":"Pull request","x":"A request to join the changes of a branch into the main branch, with review and automatic tests.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-quality-gate","t":"Quality gate","x":"A check in a pipeline that a release must pass, for example a minimum recall.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rag","t":"RAG","x":"Retrieval-augmented generation. The system adds relevant text from documents to the prompt.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-reasoning-model","t":"Reasoning model","x":"A model that works through a problem in steps before it answers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-recall","t":"Recall","x":"Of all real positive examples, the percentage that the model finds.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-recommendation-system","t":"Recommendation system","x":"A system that predicts which items a person will probably like, and shows those items first.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-regression","t":"Regression","x":"Supervised learning that predicts a number.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-relu","t":"ReLU","x":"An activation function that changes negative numbers to zero.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-reward","t":"Reward","x":"A number that tells an agent if an action was good or bad.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rlhf","t":"RLHF","x":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback. The model learns the answers that people prefer.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rnn","t":"RNN","x":"Recurrent neural network. It reads a sequence one item at a time.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-rollback","t":"Rollback","x":"A return to the previous version, to stop a problem quickly.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-sampling","t":"Sampling","x":"The selection of the next token from the calculated probabilities.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-seasonality","t":"Seasonality","x":"A pattern that repeats at the same time each day, week, or year.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-secret","t":"Secret","x":"A password, an API key, or another value that gives access to a system.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-segmentation","t":"Segmentation","x":"A task that gives a label to each pixel of an image.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-semantic-search","t":"Semantic search","x":"A search that compares the meaning of the question and the documents with embeddings.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-sentiment-analysis","t":"Sentiment analysis","x":"A task that finds if a text is positive, negative, or neutral.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-service-level-objective","t":"Service level objective","x":"A measurable target for a service, for example 99.9% availability each month.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-shadow-mode","t":"Shadow mode","x":"A test in which a new model makes predictions on real data that nobody uses yet.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-softmax","t":"Softmax","x":"A function that changes scores into probabilities with a sum of 1.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-speech-recognition","t":"Speech recognition","x":"A task that changes spoken words into text.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-standard-deviation","t":"Standard deviation","x":"The square root of the variance. The usual distance of a value from the mean.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-standardization","t":"Standardization","x":"A change of a feature so that its mean is 0 and its standard deviation is 1.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-system-prompt","t":"System prompt","x":"Instructions from the maker of an AI product that the model gets before your prompt.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-temperature","t":"Temperature","x":"A setting that controls how random the output is.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-time-series","t":"Time series","x":"A sequence of measurements in time order.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-token","t":"Token","x":"A small part of text: a word, a part of a word, or a punctuation mark.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-training","t":"Training","x":"The procedure in which a model learns from data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-transfer-learning","t":"Transfer learning","x":"Training that starts from a model that is already trained.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-transformer","t":"Transformer","x":"The neural network design that most LLMs use.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-transparency","t":"Transparency","x":"People know when AI is used and how it works.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-trend","t":"Trend","x":"The slow, long-term increase or decrease of a time series.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-underfitting","t":"Underfitting","x":"The model is too simple to find the pattern in the data.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-variable","t":"Variable","x":"A name, for example x, for a value that can change.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-variance","t":"Variance","x":"The mean of the squared differences between each value and the mean.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-vector","t":"Vector","x":"An ordered list of numbers.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-vision-transformer","t":"Vision transformer","x":"A transformer that divides an image into small squares and uses attention between them.","g":1},{"p":"glossary.html","id":"term-weight","t":"Weight","x":"An adjustable number in a model. Training changes it.","g":1}];
